WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.985 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.985 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.380 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.775 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:09.240 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.160 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: And today,
we're going to discuss

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.180 align:middle line:84%
a new topic, which is bargaining
with incomplete information.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:25.000 align:middle line:90%


00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.000 align:middle line:90%
Incomplete information.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And you recall that
earlier in the course,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.439 align:middle line:84%
we studied a model of
bargaining, but in that model,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:34.020 align:middle line:90%
we had complete information.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And when we studied bargaining
with complete information,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:45.880 align:middle line:84%
we observed-- so let's recall
that with complete info,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:54.400 align:middle line:84%
we had no disagreement
and no delay.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.360 align:middle line:84%
So when the two sides
were bargaining,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.420 align:middle line:84%
whenever there was an
agreement to be reached,

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.590 align:middle line:84%
whenever there was some
possibility for agreement,

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.150 align:middle line:84%
it was always achieved,
and it was always

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.690 align:middle line:90%
achieved in the first period.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.110 align:middle line:84%
There was never any
of this costly delay.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.350 align:middle line:84%
But of course, in reality,
we see a lot of disagreement

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.550 align:middle line:90%
and a lot of delay.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.030 align:middle line:84%
When we look at real
bargaining in practice.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.430 align:middle line:84%
And we argued that
maybe the missing piece

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.045 align:middle line:84%
of our previous model was
this very strong assumption

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.670 align:middle line:84%
of complete information,
the assumption

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.310 align:middle line:84%
that both sides exactly
understand the preferences

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.370 align:middle line:90%
of the other side.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.290 align:middle line:84%
And just to make this
a little more concrete,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.030 align:middle line:90%
let's look at an example--

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.330 align:middle line:90%
let's say plea bargaining.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:30.330 align:middle line:90%
So this is common.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.230 align:middle line:90%


00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.790 align:middle line:84%
You might have a
defendant who's accused

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:41.150 align:middle line:90%
of a crime and a prosecutor.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And before potentially
going to trial,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.197 align:middle line:84%
they might negotiate
some plea agreement which

00:01:45.197 --> 00:01:47.030 align:middle line:84%
says you don't go to
trial, and instead, you

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.790 align:middle line:90%
serve a certain amount of time.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.310 align:middle line:84%
And here, I think
there's definitely

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.750 align:middle line:84%
a role for incomplete
information.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.005 align:middle line:84%
So let's put yourself in
the role of the defendant,

00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:57.130 align:middle line:84%
and let's say you really
want to reach a plea deal,

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.690 align:middle line:84%
you don't want to go to court,
so you make a really generous

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.630 align:middle line:90%
offer to the prosecutor.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.490 align:middle line:84%
You say, look, I'll plead
guilty and I'll serve 30 years

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.750 align:middle line:90%
to avoid going to court.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.250 align:middle line:84%
Now generally you
would think making

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.230 align:middle line:84%
a very generous offer would
help you get the offer approved,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.310 align:middle line:84%
but what might the
prosecutor think if you say,

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.710 align:middle line:90%
I'm happy to serve 30 years?

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:16.493 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:02:16.493 --> 00:02:18.410 align:middle line:84%
That you're super guilty
or maybe the evidence

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.070 align:middle line:84%
is really-- yeah, the evidence
is really, really strong,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.910 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be easy
to show in court.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.970 align:middle line:84%
So there's this perverse effect
where normally a better offer is

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.610 align:middle line:84%
more likely to be accepted,
but now a better offer might

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.930 align:middle line:84%
suggest, wait a second,
there's a reason

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.510 align:middle line:84%
she's giving me a
really good offer,

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.910 align:middle line:84%
and maybe I actually
don't want to accept it

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.035 align:middle line:90%
and I'd rather go to court.

00:02:34.035 --> 00:02:36.730 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, the defendant
has private information

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:40.170 align:middle line:84%
about certainly their guilt,
but maybe more relevantly,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.490 align:middle line:84%
how easy it is to
prove their guilt.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.370 align:middle line:84%
And the defendant, their
offer maybe conveys

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.930 align:middle line:90%
information about--

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:54.285 align:middle line:84%
we'll just say guilt or evidence
of guilt or other things.

00:02:54.285 --> 00:02:55.660 align:middle line:84%
Now let's look at
the other side.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.840 align:middle line:84%
The prosecutor may also
have private information.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.960 align:middle line:84%
Let's say the prosecutor also
really wants to get a deal.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.692 align:middle line:84%
And the prosecutor
says, look, I'll

00:03:02.692 --> 00:03:04.400 align:middle line:84%
give you a really
generous deal, you only

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.180 align:middle line:84%
have to serve six months in
prison if you plead guilty,

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.840 align:middle line:84%
and then we don't
have to go to court.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.320 align:middle line:90%
That sounds like a great deal.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.520 align:middle line:84%
Why might the
defendant be hesitant

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.150 align:middle line:84%
if they received
an offer like that?

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:15.900 align:middle line:84%
A really generous offer
by the prosecutor?

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:16.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If we assume
the prosecutor doesn't

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.500 align:middle line:84%
have any evidence, so
if they go to court,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.140 align:middle line:84%
the defendant might
be able to have--

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:21.997 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:03:21.997 --> 00:03:23.580 align:middle line:84%
So this is, I think,
the key question.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.330 align:middle line:84%
They don't just say
how good is the offer,

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.380 align:middle line:84%
they say, what is the
quality of the offer?

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.180 align:middle line:84%
Tell me about the other
side's information.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.580 align:middle line:84%
And maybe they
just have no case.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.360 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I should go to
court even though this offer

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.340 align:middle line:90%
seems really good.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.200 align:middle line:84%
So here, the prosecutor's
offer might convey info

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.240 align:middle line:84%
about maybe the
evidence they have.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And I think-- legal
scholars certainly

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.000 align:middle line:84%
think this is exactly why we
often see disagreement and delay

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.720 align:middle line:84%
in bargaining because the kinds
of offers that you might make

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.190 align:middle line:84%
to try to reach agreement can
convey information that make it

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.910 align:middle line:90%
harder to reach an agreement.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:01.830 align:middle line:84%
So today, we're going to
look at two general classes

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.030 align:middle line:90%
of bargaining games.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.190 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's
helpful, at first,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:17.149 align:middle line:84%
to distinguish two different
situations, signaling

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:18.789 align:middle line:90%
and screening.

00:04:18.789 --> 00:04:21.450 align:middle line:84%
So, so far, we already
talked about signaling games

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.710 align:middle line:84%
last class where someone might
choose a level of education

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.830 align:middle line:84%
to try to signal their
ability as a worker.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.830 align:middle line:84%
And more generally, in signaling
games, what distinguishes them

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:35.650 align:middle line:84%
is that the first mover
has private information.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:41.590 align:middle line:90%


00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:43.510 align:middle line:84%
So when the first
mover, the player

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.410 align:middle line:84%
who takes an action first,
has private information,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.710 align:middle line:84%
their action is going
to convey something

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.050 align:middle line:90%
about their private information.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.750 align:middle line:84%
So their action might reveal
or convey information.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:59.527 align:middle line:90%


00:04:59.527 --> 00:05:01.110 align:middle line:84%
And when the first
mover is strategic,

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.610 align:middle line:84%
they're going to think through
how their action might convey

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.090 align:middle line:84%
information, and that's going to
affect their choice of action.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.170 align:middle line:90%
In screening.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:13.350 align:middle line:84%
It's the second mover that
has private information.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:18.438 align:middle line:90%


00:05:18.438 --> 00:05:20.230 align:middle line:84%
So you might say, oh,
well the first mover,

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.230 align:middle line:84%
they're going first, they don't
have any private information.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.230 align:middle line:84%
Private information doesn't
have much of a role.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.810 align:middle line:84%
But it still has a role
because what the first mover is

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.210 align:middle line:84%
potentially going
to do is they're

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.650 align:middle line:84%
going to choose their
action, anticipating

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.170 align:middle line:84%
that the response to their
action conveys information

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.650 align:middle line:90%
that the other side has.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:39.370 align:middle line:84%
So if I make an
opening offer to you,

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.330 align:middle line:84%
and your choice whether
to accept or reject

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.617 align:middle line:84%
that opening offer conveys to
me information that you have.

00:05:44.617 --> 00:05:46.450 align:middle line:84%
So here, they're signaling
information here,

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.750 align:middle line:84%
you might call they're kind
of fishing for information.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.120 align:middle line:84%
They're choosing an action so
that the response to that action

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.040 align:middle line:84%
conveys information
from the other party.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.580 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll say
fishing for information.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:04.200 align:middle line:90%


00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.378 align:middle line:84%
And the formal term screening--
oh, I guess the first mover,

00:06:07.378 --> 00:06:08.420 align:middle line:90%
I should say, is the one.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:12.880 align:middle line:90%


00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.760 align:middle line:84%
And the term screening
comes with the idea

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.100 align:middle line:90%
that your response--

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm the first mover, your
response to my action

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.162 align:middle line:84%
screens you and distinguishes
different players

00:06:21.162 --> 00:06:22.620 align:middle line:84%
with different
private information.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:23.870 align:middle line:90%
So it's a screening mechanism.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:25.760 align:middle line:90%
I might offer something to you.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.103 align:middle line:84%
I might say, oh, you
really want this?

00:06:28.103 --> 00:06:30.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me offer it
to you for a high price,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.520 align:middle line:84%
and that will screen
out people who actually

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.600 align:middle line:84%
want it and are willing to
pay a high price from people

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:34.180 align:middle line:90%
who are not.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.360 align:middle line:84%
That's where the term
screening comes from.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.863 align:middle line:84%
So today we're going
to study bargaining

00:06:38.863 --> 00:06:40.280 align:middle line:84%
with incomplete
information, we're

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.860 align:middle line:84%
going to look at both the cases
of signaling and screening,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.640 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to do it all
within a canonical framework

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.808 align:middle line:84%
of just a buyer and
seller negotiating

00:06:47.808 --> 00:06:48.850 align:middle line:90%
over the price of a good.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.650 align:middle line:84%
Of course, these ideas can be
applied much more generally,

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.208 align:middle line:84%
but these games are a bit
harder to analyze than the ones

00:06:54.208 --> 00:06:56.750 align:middle line:84%
we've done before, so we want
to stick to a very simple model

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.470 align:middle line:84%
to begin with, but the lessons
extend a lot more broadly.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.250 align:middle line:84%
So today, we're going to
look at a buyer and a seller.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:09.590 align:middle line:90%


00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.110 align:middle line:84%
And they're going
to be bargaining

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.670 align:middle line:84%
over the price at which
they exchange the good.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.270 align:middle line:84%
So we have our buyer
and our seller.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.618 align:middle line:84%
The buyer has some
value v for the good.

00:07:21.618 --> 00:07:23.910 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to make
different assumptions about who

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.620 align:middle line:84%
knows v and who
doesn't, but it's always

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.870 align:middle line:84%
going to be called v. So
this is how much the buyer is

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.550 align:middle line:90%
willing to pay for the good.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.350 align:middle line:90%
And the seller has some cost c.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:36.950 align:middle line:84%
I should point out here that
it's just nice to use v and c

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.430 align:middle line:84%
And think of c as a cost,
but another interpretation

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.590 align:middle line:84%
of the cost of the seller is
how much they value the item.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.770 align:middle line:84%
Because remember, the cost
c is what the seller loses

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.290 align:middle line:90%
when they give up the item.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.838 align:middle line:84%
And we can interpret that
utility loss in two ways.

00:07:49.838 --> 00:07:52.130 align:middle line:84%
Either they actually have to
produce the item at a cost

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:53.690 align:middle line:84%
in order to give it
to the other player,

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.190 align:middle line:84%
or they actually
like it themselves,

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.670 align:middle line:84%
and when they give it
up, they lose that.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.570 align:middle line:84%
So we'll call it a cost
c, but you could also

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.230 align:middle line:84%
think of it as the
seller's value,

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.050 align:middle line:84%
it's just, this way,
when I say value,

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.490 align:middle line:84%
we know we're talking
about the buyer.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:05.750 align:middle line:90%
So what are their utilities?

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:12.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, if they exchange
at a price of p,

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:17.010 align:middle line:84%
then the buyer's
utility is v minus p.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.870 align:middle line:84%
They get value v from the good
because they're exchanging,

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.370 align:middle line:84%
but they have to pay a
price p to the seller.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.090 align:middle line:84%
And then the
seller's utility is p

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:28.470 align:middle line:84%
minus c because the price p
is what goes to the seller,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.971 align:middle line:84%
the seller gets p
dollars, but has

00:08:30.971 --> 00:08:32.929 align:middle line:84%
to bear the cost c either
of producing the good

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.690 align:middle line:90%
or of losing it.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:40.890 align:middle line:84%
And then if there's no
exchange, well now, we just

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:44.440 align:middle line:84%
say each player gets utility
0 because the good is not

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.680 align:middle line:84%
exchanged and money
does not change hands.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.620 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have uB
equals 0 and uS equals 0.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:55.560 align:middle line:90%


00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:02.880 align:middle line:84%
Now let's-- to make some
progress here, at first,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.640 align:middle line:84%
let's first think about,
well, if we think we're going

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.440 align:middle line:84%
to-- if we might see exchange,
what are the possible ranges

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.880 align:middle line:84%
of prices at which
exchange takes place?

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.625 align:middle line:84%
Well, at least if v and
c are known-- so let's

00:09:17.625 --> 00:09:18.500 align:middle line:90%
start with this case.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.800 align:middle line:84%
So case 1 is kind
of the easy case.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.740 align:middle line:84%
Case 1 is where v and
c are publicly known.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.360 align:middle line:90%


00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.860 align:middle line:84%
So this is the case that we
studied earlier in the course.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.300 align:middle line:84%
This is actually a case
of complete information.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:39.260 align:middle line:90%


00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:40.760 align:middle line:84%
So let's review
this, and then we'll

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.950 align:middle line:84%
contrast it with
the other cases.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:48.210 align:middle line:84%
Well, if v and c are public,
there's something we can say.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:53.430 align:middle line:84%
If trade happens at
p, we can say a lot

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.570 align:middle line:90%
about what p must satisfy.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.230 align:middle line:84%
If v and c are public-- let's
suppose we see the players

00:09:58.230 --> 00:09:59.470 align:middle line:90%
trade at price p.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.830 align:middle line:84%
What do we know about how
p compares to v and c?

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:03.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.308 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So p has to be
greater than c, but less than v.

00:10:06.308 --> 00:10:06.850 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:07.810 align:middle line:90%
And can you explain why?

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:08.350 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:12.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The reason is that
we need the p to be at least 0,

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:14.950 align:middle line:84%
otherwise the buyer would
choose not to exchange.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:15.950 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --p minus c could
be greater than or equal to 0

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.250 align:middle line:84%
for the seller to
want to exchange.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.250 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:22.410 align:middle line:90%
So we have two constraints.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.318 align:middle line:84%
If they exchange at price p,
this is voluntary exchange.

00:10:25.318 --> 00:10:27.110 align:middle line:84%
It only happens if both
players agree to it

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.068 align:middle line:84%
because either player
can walk away and say no.

00:10:29.068 --> 00:10:31.630 align:middle line:84%
So the only possible
prices we could exchange

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:34.190 align:middle line:84%
are prices p that make
each player weakly better

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.870 align:middle line:84%
off than their outside
option of just walking away.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:40.137 align:middle line:84%
So if we trade at price p,
we must immediately have--

00:10:40.137 --> 00:10:42.220 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll write it in two
separate inequalities--

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.500 align:middle line:84%
p greater than or equal
to c and p less than

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:48.520 align:middle line:84%
or equal to v. This inequality
is because of the seller,

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:49.940 align:middle line:90%
so maybe I'll put S here.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.780 align:middle line:84%
If p were less
than c, the seller

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.220 align:middle line:84%
would never want to
trade at price p.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.620 align:middle line:90%
And this comes from the buyer.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.320 align:middle line:84%
If the price were
greater than v,

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.620 align:middle line:84%
the buyer would
simply walk away.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.940 align:middle line:84%
So we immediately see from this
that if trade is going to occur,

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.140 align:middle line:84%
we must have c
smaller than p smaller

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.340 align:middle line:84%
than v, and in particular, that
means c must be smaller than v.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:17.420 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume that
c is strictly smaller

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:18.980 align:middle line:84%
than v. This is a
common assumption

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.780 align:middle line:90%
to rule out the trivial case.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.660 align:middle line:84%
If v is strictly smaller
than c, then there's

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.900 align:middle line:84%
no way we can ever
have exchange.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.240 align:middle line:84%
And if v equals c, well,
then we could have exchange,

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.800 align:middle line:84%
but it's only going to be at
the price p equals c equals v,

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.480 align:middle line:84%
and then there's no
change in utility

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.022 align:middle line:84%
and it's not a very
interesting case.

00:11:35.022 --> 00:11:36.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is where
sometimes what we say here

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.460 align:middle line:90%
is there are gains from trade.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:42.680 align:middle line:90%


00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:45.020 align:middle line:84%
Some people say this is
what economics is all about,

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:45.883 align:middle line:90%
gains from trade.

00:11:45.883 --> 00:11:47.300 align:middle line:84%
Why are the gains
from trade here?

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.000 align:middle line:84%
Well, when the good moves
from the seller to the buyer,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.040 align:middle line:84%
the buyer's utility goes up
by v and the seller's utility

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.200 align:middle line:90%
goes down by c.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.080 align:middle line:84%
And because v is
bigger than c, it's

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.818 align:middle line:84%
possible for there
to be a price such

00:11:57.818 --> 00:11:59.360 align:middle line:84%
that both players
are better off for.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:02.000 align:middle line:84%
The gain to the buyer is bigger
than the loss to the seller

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.120 align:middle line:90%
when we exchange.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.300 align:middle line:84%
So in this case,
we've done it before,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.900 align:middle line:84%
but let's just go
through the analysis.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.440 align:middle line:84%
We need to make a choice, our
modeling choice about the order

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:13.630 align:middle line:90%
in which the players move.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:15.880 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's pretty
standard, at least in practice,

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.160 align:middle line:90%
when we see--

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.280 align:middle line:84%
certainly in stores,
we see Sellers often

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.378 align:middle line:90%
post a price first.

00:12:21.378 --> 00:12:23.420 align:middle line:84%
You could maybe walk in
and offer your own price,

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:25.500 align:middle line:84%
and in one-off negotiations,
that does happen,

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.100 align:middle line:84%
but let's think of the case
where we have many goods,

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:29.580 align:middle line:90%
we tend to see it done this way.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:34.820 align:middle line:84%
So let's make our timing be that
the seller proposes a price.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:41.830 align:middle line:90%


00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.298 align:middle line:90%
And then the buyer--

00:12:44.298 --> 00:12:46.090 align:middle line:84%
of course, it's always
the potential buyer,

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:47.550 align:middle line:84%
they don't have
to actually buy--

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:49.868 align:middle line:84%
observes the price p that's
offered, and then chooses

00:12:49.868 --> 00:12:50.910 align:middle line:90%
whether to accept or not.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:53.290 align:middle line:84%
Accept the offer or not,
meaning buy or not buy.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.630 align:middle line:90%


00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:59.450 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we'll say
buy or not buy.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.470 align:middle line:90%


00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:05.680 align:middle line:84%
And if we want to draw
this in extensive form

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.430 align:middle line:84%
just as a-- so maybe
this is a good review

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:09.250 align:middle line:84%
as we're getting
closer to the exam.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.630 align:middle line:90%
So we have the seller here.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.710 align:middle line:84%
Now it's a little tricky because
the price can take any value,

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:16.330 align:middle line:90%
say any non-negative value.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.050 align:middle line:84%
So there's infinitely many
prices the seller can offer.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.230 align:middle line:84%
It's always a little
hard to write that down,

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.670 align:middle line:84%
but maybe we'll write
it something like this.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.310 align:middle line:90%
I'll call this the price p.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.430 align:middle line:84%
I'm visualizing this as
the full range of prices

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.630 align:middle line:90%
the seller can offer.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:34.460 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put a
distinguished one here.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:40.140 align:middle line:84%
And then the buyer sees the
price and makes a binary choice,

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:42.020 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it A and
R, accept or reject.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:45.060 align:middle line:84%
Accept means buy,
reject means not buy.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.340 align:middle line:84%
And then let's make sure
we get the payoffs here.

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.500 align:middle line:84%
Remember, v and c are
known, these are just

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.820 align:middle line:90%
parameters of the game.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.580 align:middle line:84%
So if the price p is
accepted by the buyer,

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.840 align:middle line:84%
then I think we
put buyer first--

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.740 align:middle line:84%
so let's do buyer
utilities first.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.380 align:middle line:84%
The buyer is going to get
v minus p, the seller is

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.115 align:middle line:90%
going to get p minus c.

00:14:05.115 --> 00:14:06.740 align:middle line:84%
And over here, it's
just going to be 0,

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:08.880 align:middle line:90%
0 because both sides reject.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:12.380 align:middle line:90%


00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's try to
understand, let's try

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.620 align:middle line:84%
to solve for a subgame perfect
Nash equilibrium of this game.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:24.370 align:middle line:84%
Notice, we can use the solution
concept of subgame perfect Nash

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.620 align:middle line:84%
because there's no private
information because this is

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.120 align:middle line:90%
a game of complete information.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.820 align:middle line:84%
Once we move to
incomplete information,

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:32.100 align:middle line:84%
we have to use perfect
Bayesian equilibrium,

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.440 align:middle line:84%
but for this, we can just
use subgame perfect Nash.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:38.520 align:middle line:84%
So let's look for subgame
perfect Nash equilibrium.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:39.920 align:middle line:90%
What we want to do?

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.533 align:middle line:84%
Well, the trick with
a game like this

00:14:41.533 --> 00:14:42.700 align:middle line:90%
is we always work backwards.

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:45.340 align:middle line:84%
In fact with this game, we
can apply backward induction.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:48.160 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with
the second player.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.920 align:middle line:90%
This is the buyer.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:55.640 align:middle line:84%
And we can ask,
well, what offers

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:58.000 align:middle line:90%
is the buyer going to accept?

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.502 align:middle line:84%
Well, the buyer can either
get 0 or take the offer.

00:15:01.502 --> 00:15:02.960 align:middle line:84%
So they're going
to accept an offer

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.080 align:middle line:84%
if their total utility
from accepting the offer

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.720 align:middle line:90%
is at least 0.

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:10.120 align:middle line:84%
So the buyer is
going to accept--

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:12.440 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll use a double
arrow to say if and only

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:18.000 align:middle line:84%
if v minus p is greater
than or equal to 0.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.600 align:middle line:84%
This is the utility of
accepting the offer,

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.740 align:middle line:84%
this is their utility
of rejecting the offer,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.280 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to accept
if the utility from accepting

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:27.320 align:middle line:84%
is better than the
utility of rejecting.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:30.230 align:middle line:84%
There's a little flexibility
if this equals exactly 0,

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:34.090 align:middle line:84%
but a common theme, when
you're looking for equilibria,

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:35.790 align:middle line:84%
it's often better to
break ties this way

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.582 align:middle line:84%
and say that when
indifferent, they accept.

00:15:37.582 --> 00:15:42.270 align:middle line:84%
It tends to make sure the
sender deals with some existence

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:44.887 align:middle line:84%
problems for the
sender's choice.

00:15:44.887 --> 00:15:47.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, another way of saying this
is they're going to accept if

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:50.710 align:middle line:84%
and only if v is greater
than or equal to p.

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.710 align:middle line:84%
And anticipating
that, we can now

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.450 align:middle line:84%
go to the first mover,
the sender-- the seller.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:00.132 align:middle line:90%


00:16:00.132 --> 00:16:01.590 align:middle line:84%
And what is the
seller going to do?

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.550 align:middle line:84%
If they anticipate the buyer
will accept if and only

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:06.870 align:middle line:84%
if v is greater than or
equal to p, well, then,

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.295 align:middle line:84%
what price do they
want to offer?

00:16:09.295 --> 00:16:10.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well,
wouldn't you just

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:11.950 align:middle line:84%
offer the highest
price possible?

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:14.408 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The highest price
possible that will be accepted.

00:16:14.408 --> 00:16:18.350 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, that's
just p equals v. Notice here,

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.782 align:middle line:84%
we're using our assumption
that c is less than v.

00:16:21.782 --> 00:16:23.990 align:middle line:84%
Because if v was less than
c, they might not actually

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.890 align:middle line:84%
want to offer v because that
wouldn't cover their costs,

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.980 align:middle line:84%
but under our assumption that
v is at least as big as c, then

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.660 align:middle line:84%
the seller is going to make--
offer the highest price that

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:34.980 align:middle line:84%
will be accepted,
that's p equals v,

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.680 align:middle line:84%
and then we can see what are
the utilities going to be.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.060 align:middle line:90%


00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:44.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, the buyer is going to
get v minus v. The first v

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:45.200 align:middle line:90%
is their value.

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.300 align:middle line:84%
The second v is actually the
price, but it equals the value,

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:50.260 align:middle line:90%
so that's going to be 0.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:57.160 align:middle line:84%
And the sender is
going to get v minus c.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:00.360 align:middle line:84%
v here is playing the role
of the price minus the cost,

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.920 align:middle line:84%
and this is strictly
greater than 0.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:06.060 align:middle line:90%


00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:09.359 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes terminology you might
say, remember, we called v--

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.740 align:middle line:84%
v minus c, we said
there's gains from trade

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:13.339 align:middle line:90%
because v is greater than c.

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:15.819 align:middle line:84%
We sometimes think of v minus
c as the potential gains

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:16.640 align:middle line:90%
from trade.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.900 align:middle line:84%
This is the amount of utility
that can go up if they trade.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:22.380 align:middle line:84%
And here, we sometimes
say the seller has fully

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:23.880 align:middle line:90%
extracted the gains from trade.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.338 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll say
this-- or maybe I'll

00:17:25.338 --> 00:17:29.180 align:middle line:84%
say the seller gets
all gains from trade.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:33.643 align:middle line:90%


00:17:33.643 --> 00:17:35.560 align:middle line:84%
In this game, we can
think of this-- remember,

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.240 align:middle line:84%
before we studied this
game, split the dollar.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:39.780 align:middle line:84%
v minus c is
basically the dollar.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.160 align:middle line:84%
This is the potential gains
that are on the table.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.560 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be split in
some way between the buyer

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:48.280 align:middle line:84%
and the seller, and in this
case, the buyer gets none of it

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:49.940 align:middle line:90%
and the seller gets all of this.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.295 align:middle line:84%
And this is because the
seller gets to move first,

00:17:52.295 --> 00:17:54.420 align:middle line:84%
anticipating the way the
buyer is going to respond.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:18:03.400 align:middle line:90%


00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.260 align:middle line:84%
So now that we've
reviewed this case,

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:09.020 align:middle line:84%
let's now introduce
incomplete information.

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.240 align:middle line:84%
So let's make different
assumptions about v and c,

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:14.920 align:middle line:84%
unless there's any
questions on this.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:16.892 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to start with--

00:18:16.892 --> 00:18:18.600 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned over here,
there's two cases,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:19.700 align:middle line:90%
signaling and screening.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:21.140 align:middle line:90%
And let's be really clear here.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:24.030 align:middle line:84%
This game didn't involve
any signaling or screening

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:26.450 align:middle line:84%
because the seller and the
buyer-- or in this case,

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.870 align:middle line:84%
the seller didn't have
any private information.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:31.185 align:middle line:84%
So the price they offered
didn't convey anything,

00:18:31.185 --> 00:18:33.310 align:middle line:84%
and this didn't fit into
our framework of signaling

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:36.110 align:middle line:84%
and screening, but now we're
going to move into that case.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:39.450 align:middle line:90%
So let's start with screening.

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:46.790 align:middle line:90%


00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to imagine
is that the buyer--

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:59.670 align:middle line:84%
we're still going to assume
that the seller makes an offer,

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:01.670 align:middle line:84%
but now we're going to
assume that the buyer has

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:02.910 align:middle line:90%
private information.

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:04.410 align:middle line:90%
Buyer, but not seller.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:14.910 align:middle line:90%


00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:16.990 align:middle line:84%
So let's be a
little more precise.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:19.410 align:middle line:84%
The seller doesn't have
any private information,

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.682 align:middle line:84%
so let's assume that the cost c
of the seller is publicly known.

00:19:22.682 --> 00:19:24.640 align:middle line:84%
This is commonly known,
everyone observes this.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:30.300 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say the
cost c is known--

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:31.280 align:middle line:90%
or is public.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.980 align:middle line:90%


00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.640 align:middle line:84%
What's not known is
the buyer's valuation,

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.580 align:middle line:84%
how much the buyer
values the good.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.280 align:middle line:84%
But v is private
information of the buyer.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:53.700 align:middle line:90%


00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:55.380 align:middle line:84%
So this fits into
our screening setting

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:58.340 align:middle line:84%
because it's the second
mover, the buyer who

00:19:58.340 --> 00:19:59.880 align:middle line:90%
has the private information.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.580 align:middle line:84%
The first mover,
the seller, doesn't

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:03.080 align:middle line:90%
have any private information.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:06.140 align:middle line:84%
But as we said before, the
second mover's response

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.160 align:middle line:84%
to the first mover's action is
going to convey information.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:12.660 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the first
mover's action by the seller

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.620 align:middle line:90%
is to offer a price.

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:17.020 align:middle line:84%
The buyer, then, is going
to choose whether or not

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:18.420 align:middle line:90%
to buy at that price.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.780 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to reveal
information about the buyer,

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:22.680 align:middle line:84%
and in particular, it's
going to screen or separate

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:24.360 align:middle line:84%
different kinds of
buyers because buyers

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:25.760 align:middle line:84%
who value the good
more are going

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.593 align:middle line:84%
to choose to accept the
offer and buyers who

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:31.160 align:middle line:84%
value the good less are going
to choose not to accept it.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:33.500 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to model this.

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:37.283 align:middle line:84%
Well, If c is known, that's just
a number that everyone knows.

00:20:37.283 --> 00:20:39.200 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to say
anything more about that.

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.600 align:middle line:84%
But v, because it's private
information of the buyer,

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:45.120 align:middle line:84%
we have to specify the seller's
beliefs about this quantity v.

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:47.580 align:middle line:84%
So let's make our
standard assumption.

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:50.580 align:middle line:84%
Let's say that v is
uniformly distributed--

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:52.360 align:middle line:84%
this just makes the
algebra a bit easier--

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:58.120 align:middle line:90%
is uniform over 0, 1.

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:01.760 align:middle line:84%
It's a random variable, it's
uniformly distributed over 0, 1.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.600 align:middle line:84%
And now to make
things interesting,

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.080 align:middle line:84%
let's assume that c,
this public number,

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.780 align:middle line:90%
is somewhere between 0 and 1.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.528 align:middle line:84%
Because if c was greater than
1, well, we'd never have trade,

00:21:10.528 --> 00:21:12.820 align:middle line:84%
and if c was less than 0,
well, we'd always have trade,

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:13.960 align:middle line:90%
it's not so interesting.

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:19.002 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume c
greater than 0 is known.

00:21:19.002 --> 00:21:20.210 align:middle line:90%
And I want to highlight this.

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:21.918 align:middle line:84%
I think it's often a
source of confusion,

00:21:21.918 --> 00:21:23.833 align:middle line:84%
when people see a
variable, a letter,

00:21:23.833 --> 00:21:25.250 align:middle line:84%
they think somehow
it's not known.

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:26.490 align:middle line:84%
It's just playing
the role of a number.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:27.240 align:middle line:90%
Everyone knows it.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.990 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's 0.3, maybe
it's 0.7, but we're just

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.390 align:middle line:84%
using a abstract
symbol c to make

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:33.730 align:middle line:90%
it things a little bit cleaner.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:34.322 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:21:34.322 --> 00:21:36.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does the seller
note the distribution of v?

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:37.113 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes.

00:21:37.113 --> 00:21:39.390 align:middle line:84%
So when we say
that v is uniform,

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:41.510 align:middle line:84%
everything we're saying
here is common knowledge

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:42.290 align:middle line:90%
among the players.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:43.915 align:middle line:84%
So not only is it
distributed according

00:21:43.915 --> 00:21:46.630 align:middle line:84%
to the uniform distribution,
but if we're really precise,

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:48.670 align:middle line:84%
the seller knows that, the buyer
knows that the seller knows

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:51.295 align:middle line:84%
that, and so on, everything I've
said here is common knowledge.

00:21:51.295 --> 00:21:54.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question OK.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:59.850 align:middle line:90%


00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:00.350 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.670 align:middle line:84%
So now let's-- ah, c greater
than 0, and I also see--

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:07.650 align:middle line:84%
let me be more precise, it's
0 less than c less than 1.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.990 align:middle line:90%


00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:13.790 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now let's look
at the seller's problem.

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:16.400 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose the seller
offers a price of p.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:20.980 align:middle line:90%


00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.788 align:middle line:84%
Well, if they offer a price
of p, who's going to buy?

00:22:23.788 --> 00:22:25.580 align:middle line:84%
Well, the buyer is
going to buy if and only

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.220 align:middle line:90%
if their value is at least p.

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:33.300 align:middle line:84%
So then buyer accepts or
buys-- kind of use those

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.780 align:middle line:90%
interchangeably--

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:41.780 align:middle line:84%
if and only if v is
greater than or equal to p.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:43.900 align:middle line:84%
And let's be a little
more precise here.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:46.500 align:middle line:84%
What kind of price might
the seller reasonably offer?

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:48.760 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't make sense for
them to offer more than 1.

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:50.240 align:middle line:84%
If the price they
charge is more than 1,

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:51.620 align:middle line:90%
it's never going to be accepted.

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:53.020 align:middle line:84%
And if it's less
than 0, well, that

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:54.280 align:middle line:84%
means they're paying
you to get the good,

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:55.280 align:middle line:90%
that doesn't make sense.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:59.038 align:middle line:84%
So let's focus on a price
offer that's between 0 and 1.

00:22:59.038 --> 00:23:00.580 align:middle line:84%
And we can show that
they would never

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:04.080 align:middle line:84%
want to offer a price
outside that range.

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:07.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, the buyer is going
to accept or buy--

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.780 align:middle line:84%
slash, buy-- if and only if v
is greater than or equal to p.

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.500 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's think
about how likely this

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.320 align:middle line:84%
is, because as the
seller, all I care about

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:17.080 align:middle line:84%
is when I offer you a price
of p, what is the probability

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:18.960 align:middle line:84%
that you actually
buy at that price?

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:23.000 align:middle line:84%
So we can think-- we can
draw a little graph--

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:26.720 align:middle line:84%
here's 0, here's 1,
the buyer's value

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.280 align:middle line:84%
is uniformly distributed
over this interval,

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:34.200 align:middle line:84%
the seller is choosing some
price p in this interval,

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.240 align:middle line:84%
and the buyer is going
to buy if and only

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.820 align:middle line:90%
if their valuation lies above p.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.840 align:middle line:90%
So lies in this segment.

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.360 align:middle line:84%
Because we're working with
the uniform distribution,

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:47.003 align:middle line:84%
the probability that the buyer's
value lies in this segment

00:23:47.003 --> 00:23:48.420 align:middle line:84%
is just the length
of the segment.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.600 align:middle line:84%
That's a nice, convenient
property of the uniform.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:55.220 align:middle line:84%
So the probability
here is just 1 minus p.

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:58.520 align:middle line:90%


00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:04.480 align:middle line:84%
So now, we can compute
what is the seller's profit

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.400 align:middle line:90%
from offering a price of p?

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, as usual, profit is
the number or the probability

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.350 align:middle line:84%
that you sell the
item times the profit

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:13.750 align:middle line:90%
you get when you do sell it.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:16.070 align:middle line:84%
So if they do sell
it, their profit

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:20.790 align:middle line:84%
is going to be p minus c because
the buyer buys at price p,

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.510 align:middle line:84%
pays p to the
seller, but remember,

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.470 align:middle line:84%
the seller has to pay
a cost c to sell it.

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:27.670 align:middle line:84%
But we have to multiply
that by the probability

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:30.430 align:middle line:84%
that the buyer
actually sells it--

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.910 align:middle line:84%
or that the buyer
actually buys it, sorry,

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:35.990 align:middle line:84%
which is, here, the
probability that v

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:37.750 align:middle line:90%
is greater than or equal to p.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:43.130 align:middle line:84%
And from what we said down
here, this is just 1 minus p.

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:46.870 align:middle line:90%


00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:49.670 align:middle line:84%
So as usual, we have the
two counteracting forces.

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:54.110 align:middle line:84%
When we raise the price
p, "we" being the seller,

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:58.510 align:middle line:84%
we earn more per item, but we
reduce the probability that

00:24:58.510 --> 00:24:59.910 align:middle line:90%
the item is purchased.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:01.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
fundamental trade-off.

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:02.690 align:middle line:84%
If you set the
price really high,

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:04.565 align:middle line:84%
it's great when someone
buys, but they're not

00:25:04.565 --> 00:25:05.790 align:middle line:90%
very likely to buy.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.430 align:middle line:84%
And if we just use calculus
or use our trick here,

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:15.120 align:middle line:84%
we can see that the solution
is going to be 1 plus c over 2.

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:22.180 align:middle line:90%


00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:26.420 align:middle line:84%
Now I've framed everything here,
and let's just do a sanity check

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:27.500 align:middle line:90%
as we always do.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:29.800 align:middle line:84%
If c increases, the
price is increasing,

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:31.180 align:middle line:90%
and that should make sense.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:33.880 align:middle line:84%
If the cost if something is
more expensive to produce,

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.500 align:middle line:84%
the price of producing it-- the
price that the seller charges

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:38.540 align:middle line:90%
is going to be higher.

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:40.660 align:middle line:90%
Now this all seemed maybe--

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.480 align:middle line:84%
we talked-- we used some fancy
words, incomplete information,

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:46.140 align:middle line:84%
but it turns out, this is
really equivalent to a very

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:48.440 align:middle line:84%
classical kind of
Econ 101 problem,

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:51.340 align:middle line:84%
and I want to show
the connection here.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:56.380 align:middle line:84%
So I know some of you have
taken econ, maybe some of you

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.820 align:middle line:84%
haven't, but if we just look at
the classic supply and demand

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:07.330 align:middle line:84%
curve, we have price on this
axis and quantity on this axis.

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:11.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can think of a seller
facing a buyer like this

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:19.190 align:middle line:84%
as facing a demand curve
that looks like this.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:23.770 align:middle line:90%


00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:27.610 align:middle line:84%
Because if I'm the seller,
whatever price p I offer,

00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:30.370 align:middle line:84%
we already said from our
calculation over here

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:34.050 align:middle line:84%
that the buyer is going to buy
with probability 1 minus p.

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.890 align:middle line:84%
So I'm effectively
facing a demand curve

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:39.450 align:middle line:90%
that looks like this.

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:42.730 align:middle line:84%
If I charge a price of
1, the buyer never buys,

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:44.570 align:middle line:90%
so demand is 0.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:47.770 align:middle line:84%
If I set a price of
0, the buyer always

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.910 align:middle line:84%
buys, which means their
probability of buying is just 1.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:52.410 align:middle line:90%
That's my demand here.

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:56.630 align:middle line:84%
And if I set an intermediate
price of p, well,

00:26:56.630 --> 00:26:59.510 align:middle line:84%
then the buyer buys with
probability 1 minus p,

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:01.310 align:middle line:84%
and it's this point
along the demand curve.

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:04.650 align:middle line:84%
So as usual, in economics, we
see a downward-sloping demand

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:05.330 align:middle line:90%
curve.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:08.230 align:middle line:84%
The lower the price I offer
as the seller, the more likely

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:09.390 align:middle line:90%
the buyer is to buy.

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.190 align:middle line:84%
Now I'll highlight-- I'll
say up front that normally we

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:15.290 align:middle line:84%
think of q as the number
of units purchased,

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:16.910 align:middle line:90%
not the probability of purchase.

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:20.050 align:middle line:84%
But if we're facing a population
of, say, 100 people, well,

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:22.430 align:middle line:84%
we can reinterpret this
probability where a 1/2

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:25.390 align:middle line:84%
probability of buying now
means, in expectation,

00:27:25.390 --> 00:27:28.090 align:middle line:84%
50 out of 100 people in
your town are going to buy.

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:30.390 align:middle line:84%
So we can reinterpret
quantity as--

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.270 align:middle line:84%
let's reinterpret it here
as probability of buying

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:38.410 align:middle line:90%
times the number of people.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:47.190 align:middle line:90%


00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:49.293 align:middle line:84%
Up here, I just assumed
there was a single buyer,

00:27:49.293 --> 00:27:50.710 align:middle line:84%
but the math doesn't
really change

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:54.170 align:middle line:84%
if you just multiply everything
by 100-- say there's 100 people.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.888 align:middle line:84%
And here, we see the connection
with-- if you've taken econ

00:27:56.888 --> 00:27:58.930 align:middle line:84%
and we talk about the
monopolist pricing problem,

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:00.830 align:middle line:84%
this is exactly
what they're doing.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:02.970 align:middle line:84%
Of course, I need one
more thing on the figure,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:06.860 align:middle line:84%
I need the cost, because that's
also relevant to the monopolist.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:10.060 align:middle line:90%
So let me put the cost c here.

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:19.700 align:middle line:84%
And what we can see is
this box here represents

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.020 align:middle line:90%
the monopolist profit.

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:23.120 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:26.620 align:middle line:84%
Well, the height of
the box is p minus c.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:30.300 align:middle line:84%
That's what we had up here
for the profit per unit.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:34.100 align:middle line:84%
The width of the
box is 1 minus p.

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:36.860 align:middle line:84%
It's determined by the
value of the demand curve

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:39.780 align:middle line:84%
at the price p that's
offered, so that represents

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.860 align:middle line:90%
the probability of sale.

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:43.940 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, what's
the seller's goal?

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:45.860 align:middle line:84%
They want to move
this price up and down

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:48.140 align:middle line:84%
and choose the value
that makes this box have

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:50.540 align:middle line:90%
the largest possible area.

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:52.660 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
this is maximized

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:54.837 align:middle line:90%
when the box is a square.

00:28:54.837 --> 00:28:56.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's actually
what happens here.

00:28:56.420 --> 00:29:02.530 align:middle line:84%
When p star equals 1
plus c over 2, then--

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:04.610 align:middle line:90%
let's just observe this.

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:09.850 align:middle line:84%
If p star equals
1 plus c over 2,

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:24.490 align:middle line:84%
then that means p star minus
c equals 1 minus c over 2.

00:29:24.490 --> 00:29:26.250 align:middle line:90%
So I have 1 plus c over 2.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:29.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm subtracting c, but
since I'm dividing by 2,

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:34.770 align:middle line:84%
I can subtract 2c on the top,
and then I get 1 minus c over 2.

00:29:34.770 --> 00:29:38.770 align:middle line:90%
And then what is my demand?

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:46.510 align:middle line:84%
1 minus-- oh, did I do
something wrong here?

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:56.350 align:middle line:90%


00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:56.850 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:29:56.850 --> 00:29:59.790 align:middle line:90%


00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:01.830 align:middle line:84%
We know what the answer
has to be-- let's see.

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:04.190 align:middle line:84%
We'll write it out
more carefully.

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:14.110 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can write this as 2
over 2 minus 1 plus c over 2.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:15.050 align:middle line:90%
And now it's clear.

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:18.170 align:middle line:84%
2 minus 1 is 1, minus c,
we get 1 minus c over 2.

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:21.350 align:middle line:90%


00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:23.110 align:middle line:90%
So this is at p star.

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:26.110 align:middle line:84%
The height of the box
is 1 minus c over 2,

00:30:26.110 --> 00:30:28.390 align:middle line:84%
and the width of the box
is also 1 minus c over 2,

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:30.240 align:middle line:90%
and we get a square.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.490 align:middle line:84%
That's a special property
of the uniform distribution.

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.830 align:middle line:84%
So one final comment
is, when we drew

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:37.070 align:middle line:84%
the connection between
the demand curve

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:39.670 align:middle line:84%
figure and the incomplete
information figure,

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:41.990 align:middle line:84%
the distribution of
the buyer's value

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:44.710 align:middle line:84%
exactly corresponds to the
shape of the demand curve.

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.150 align:middle line:84%
So when we draw different
demand curves in econ classes,

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:49.190 align:middle line:84%
one interpretation of
that is it corresponds

00:30:49.190 --> 00:30:52.270 align:middle line:84%
to different distributions
of values in the population.

00:30:52.270 --> 00:30:54.740 align:middle line:84%
A demand curve
shifting out means

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:56.990 align:middle line:84%
more people are willing to
buy at a given price, which

00:30:56.990 --> 00:30:59.260 align:middle line:84%
means the distribution of
values in the population

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:00.700 align:middle line:90%
tends to be higher.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:02.920 align:middle line:84%
OK, but this was
all still a review.

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:07.820 align:middle line:84%
Let's now move into the
incomplete information case--

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:10.280 align:middle line:84%
or the more complicated
incomplete information case.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:12.960 align:middle line:90%
So let's go back over here.

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:28.940 align:middle line:90%


00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:32.500 align:middle line:84%
So before, what we
assumed is that--

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:35.520 align:middle line:84%
well, one question here is to
say, why does the buyer care?

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:38.595 align:middle line:84%
Why does the seller care
about the buyer's valuation?

00:31:38.595 --> 00:31:40.220 align:middle line:84%
They care because
the buyer's valuation

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:42.260 align:middle line:90%
affects whether they buy.

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:44.860 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't affect how
costly it is for the seller

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:46.300 align:middle line:90%
to produce the item.

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:49.820 align:middle line:84%
But there's a lot of cases
where the buyer's private

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:51.620 align:middle line:90%
information-- so let's say--

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:53.420 align:middle line:90%
I'll say this is another case.

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:55.140 align:middle line:84%
It's the case of
interdependent--

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:59.050 align:middle line:84%
sometimes called interdependent
values in the notes.

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:05.650 align:middle line:90%
Interdependent values.

00:32:05.650 --> 00:32:13.450 align:middle line:84%
And this is the case where the
buyer's private information

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.690 align:middle line:84%
is relevant not just to how
much the buyer values the good,

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:19.570 align:middle line:84%
but the cost to the seller
of providing the good.

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:24.070 align:middle line:84%
So the buyer's private
information is about v and c.

00:32:24.070 --> 00:32:26.817 align:middle line:90%


00:32:26.817 --> 00:32:28.150 align:middle line:90%
And this might be a bit strange.

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:30.483 align:middle line:84%
Why would the buyer know how
costly it is for the seller

00:32:30.483 --> 00:32:31.650 align:middle line:90%
to produce the item?

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:35.050 align:middle line:84%
But there's a few
classic cases here.

00:32:35.050 --> 00:32:37.010 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone know what's
a classic case where

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.130 align:middle line:84%
the buyer might have
private information that's

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:41.090 align:middle line:84%
relevant to both what
the buyer gets out

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.770 align:middle line:84%
of the exchange
and also the cost

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:46.350 align:middle line:84%
to the seller of
making the exchange?

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:50.290 align:middle line:90%


00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:51.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:52.675 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it
possible that the buyer

00:32:52.675 --> 00:32:54.910 align:middle line:84%
would value the good even
more and know that it's

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:57.150 align:middle line:90%
costs more [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:59.430 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, that's--

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:03.270 align:middle line:90%
I think that's possible, yes.

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:06.070 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think that's along
the right track, yeah.

00:33:06.070 --> 00:33:08.230 align:middle line:84%
There's one
classical application

00:33:08.230 --> 00:33:10.470 align:middle line:90%
that everyone talks about.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:11.630 align:middle line:90%
So this is health care.

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:14.092 align:middle line:90%
Health insurance.

00:33:14.092 --> 00:33:15.550 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll give
one more example

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:18.270 align:middle line:84%
that's a bit closer
to what you suggested.

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:19.010 align:middle line:90%
Health insurance.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:22.230 align:middle line:84%
What does the buyer know
that's relevant to both v and c

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:24.610 align:middle line:84%
in the context of
health insurance?

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:25.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:26.030 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Their own status?

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:27.447 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Their
own health status.

00:33:27.447 --> 00:33:29.070 align:middle line:84%
The buyer is, here,
an individual,

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:30.470 align:middle line:90%
they're buying insurance.

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:33.030 align:middle line:84%
This is one reason why we see
the government quite involved

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:34.550 align:middle line:84%
in health insurance,
and the reason

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:37.250 align:middle line:84%
why health insurance markets,
unfettered private markets,

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:39.670 align:middle line:84%
tend not to work well,
precisely because of this issue.

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:42.950 align:middle line:84%
Because the buyer has private
information that's relevant

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:45.170 align:middle line:84%
both to how much they
want the insurance,

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:47.550 align:middle line:84%
but also to how costly
it is for the seller

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:49.350 align:middle line:90%
to provide insurance to them.

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:59.160 align:middle line:84%
So what the buyer knows
their health status.

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:01.860 align:middle line:90%


00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:03.680 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this is a
gross simplification,

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:05.222 align:middle line:84%
but in short, you
might think someone

00:34:05.222 --> 00:34:07.960 align:middle line:84%
who's healthy has a
lower v and a lower c.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:10.780 align:middle line:84%
If I'm healthy, I don't care
about insurance as much.

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:13.245 align:middle line:84%
And also, insurance is
cheaper to provide to me

00:34:13.245 --> 00:34:14.620 align:middle line:84%
because I rarely
go to the doctor

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:17.219 align:middle line:84%
and the insurance company
doesn't have to pay much.

00:34:17.219 --> 00:34:20.540 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, someone
who's quite unhealthy

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:22.435 align:middle line:84%
is going to value
insurance a lot more.

00:34:22.435 --> 00:34:24.060 align:middle line:84%
They're going to go
to the doctor a lot

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:25.520 align:middle line:84%
and get a lot more
out of the insurance,

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:27.812 align:middle line:84%
but that's also going to be
more costly for the insurer

00:34:27.812 --> 00:34:31.020 align:middle line:84%
because the insurer has to pay
every time every time they go.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, health insurance
markets sometimes

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:36.933 align:middle line:84%
try to mitigate this effect by
offering different prices based

00:34:36.933 --> 00:34:38.600 align:middle line:84%
on different observable
characteristics.

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:40.400 align:middle line:84%
It may be that
based on your price,

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:41.931 align:middle line:84%
older people tend
to charge more--

00:34:41.931 --> 00:34:44.139 align:middle line:84%
charge more for health
insurance than younger people,

00:34:44.139 --> 00:34:46.179 align:middle line:84%
but there's still usually
additional private information

00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:46.679 align:middle line:90%
you have.

00:34:46.679 --> 00:34:49.199 align:middle line:84%
Not all people who are 75
have exactly the same health,

00:34:49.199 --> 00:34:50.907 align:middle line:84%
and they usually have
private information

00:34:50.907 --> 00:34:53.530 align:middle line:90%
about how healthy they are.

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:55.770 align:middle line:84%
So this can create a
problem, which is often

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:58.940 align:middle line:90%
known as adverse selection.

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:01.190 align:middle line:84%
And this term actually--
it's used in economics a lot,

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:03.410 align:middle line:84%
but it actually was
first-- the term was first

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:05.410 align:middle line:84%
coined by people who
worked in insurance

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.010 align:middle line:84%
and noted this phenomenon, and
then economists subsequently

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:10.050 align:middle line:90%
studied it.

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:16.210 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at quite, maybe,
a stylized model of this.

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:19.030 align:middle line:84%
So let's represent the
population or the buyer.

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:22.410 align:middle line:90%


00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:25.890 align:middle line:84%
The cost of providing
insurance to the buyer

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.150 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
uniform over 0, 1.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:32.290 align:middle line:90%


00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:34.370 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of
c as being a measure

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.770 align:middle line:90%
of the health of the buyer.

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:38.930 align:middle line:84%
I guess a higher
c is someone who

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:41.370 align:middle line:84%
is less healthy because it's
more costly to provide them

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:42.997 align:middle line:90%
insurance.

00:35:42.997 --> 00:35:45.330 align:middle line:84%
And then we want there to be
some relationship between c

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:50.990 align:middle line:84%
and v. So let's assume that v
is equal to alpha plus beta c

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:56.710 align:middle line:84%
where alpha is greater
than or equal to 0

00:35:56.710 --> 00:36:00.250 align:middle line:84%
and beta is strictly
greater than 0.

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:04.310 align:middle line:90%


00:36:04.310 --> 00:36:07.710 align:middle line:84%
So maybe it's helpful
to draw a picture here

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.590 align:middle line:90%
to see what's going on.

00:36:09.590 --> 00:36:13.230 align:middle line:84%
We have c, we have
v. c is uniformly

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:21.670 align:middle line:84%
distributed between 0 and 1, and
v is alpha plus beta times c.

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So let's put alpha here
and alpha plus beta here.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:33.110 align:middle line:84%
And notice that when c
equals 0, v equals alpha;

00:36:33.110 --> 00:36:37.070 align:middle line:84%
and when c equals 1, v
equals alpha plus beta.

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:40.710 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of
people as lying along

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:47.680 align:middle line:90%
this line that looks like this.

00:36:47.680 --> 00:36:50.597 align:middle line:90%


00:36:50.597 --> 00:36:52.180 align:middle line:84%
We could have a more
complicated model

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:55.642 align:middle line:84%
where maybe your cost doesn't
directly pin down your value

00:36:55.642 --> 00:36:57.600 align:middle line:84%
and vice versa, there
might be some noise here,

00:36:57.600 --> 00:36:59.360 align:middle line:90%
some individual heterogeneity.

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:01.000 align:middle line:84%
But here in this
really simple model,

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.180 align:middle line:90%
everyone lives along this line.

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:05.980 align:middle line:84%
Here, we have the
healthiest people

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:08.960 align:middle line:84%
who only get a value of
alpha from the insurance,

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:10.580 align:middle line:90%
but only cost the insurer 0.

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:12.080 align:middle line:84%
And then as you
move along the line,

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:15.380 align:middle line:84%
we have the least
healthy people who

00:37:15.380 --> 00:37:17.480 align:middle line:84%
cost one to the
insurer to insure,

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:19.807 align:middle line:84%
but get utility alpha plus
beta from the insurer.

00:37:19.807 --> 00:37:22.140 align:middle line:84%
In reality, we might need a
two-dimensional model, maybe

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.580 align:middle line:84%
health and how rich you are,
and maybe everything would

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:26.160 align:middle line:84%
be filled in and we'd
have a distribution,

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.035 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to
restrict just to this line

00:37:28.035 --> 00:37:30.160 align:middle line:90%
to make things concrete.

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:32.385 align:middle line:84%
And because c is
uniform, that means

00:37:32.385 --> 00:37:34.260 align:middle line:84%
people-- we're imagining
people are uniformly

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:36.220 align:middle line:90%
distributed along this line.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:36.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:38.803 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just trying to
understand qualitatively.

00:37:38.803 --> 00:37:40.540 align:middle line:84%
How can someone
did a lot of value

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:44.770 align:middle line:84%
out of the insurer, but not
cost a lot or vice versa?

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:46.690 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It could
be-- so wealth

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:48.250 align:middle line:90%
would be the easiest case.

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:51.950 align:middle line:84%
So someone who is really rich
might be willing to pay a lot.

00:37:51.950 --> 00:37:54.790 align:middle line:84%
So what is value-- yeah, it
gets into what does value mean?

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:57.490 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I go to the
doctor and fix my toothache.

00:37:57.490 --> 00:37:59.868 align:middle line:84%
How much am I willing to
pay to fix my toothache?

00:37:59.868 --> 00:38:01.910 align:middle line:84%
I might be willing to pay
less than someone else,

00:38:01.910 --> 00:38:03.830 align:middle line:84%
even though it's
fixing the toothache.

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:05.830 align:middle line:84%
So the cost to the
insurer is just literally,

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:09.030 align:middle line:84%
how much does the doctor
get paid, and the facility,

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:10.088 align:middle line:90%
to perform the operation?

00:38:10.088 --> 00:38:12.130 align:middle line:84%
But certainly, different
people in the population

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:14.130 align:middle line:84%
would be willing to pay
very different amounts

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:15.185 align:middle line:90%
for the same operation.

00:38:15.185 --> 00:38:17.810 align:middle line:84%
A billionaire would tend to be
willing to pay more than someone

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:19.810 align:middle line:90%
who doesn't have any money.

00:38:19.810 --> 00:38:23.352 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, we're not
taking into account that effect.

00:38:23.352 --> 00:38:24.810 align:middle line:84%
We're looking at
a simplified model

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:28.810 align:middle line:84%
where there's a one-to-one
relationship between cost

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:30.533 align:middle line:90%
and value.

00:38:30.533 --> 00:38:32.450 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, I guess the
way to think about value

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:36.250 align:middle line:84%
is value is about your
individual willingness to pay,

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:38.930 align:middle line:84%
because remember,
going back to here,

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:40.970 align:middle line:84%
this is almost the
definition of value.

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:42.550 align:middle line:84%
If your value is
v, it means you're

00:38:42.550 --> 00:38:45.412 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay any amount
up to v to get that item.

00:38:45.412 --> 00:38:47.870 align:middle line:84%
And some people are willing to
pay a lot more for something

00:38:47.870 --> 00:38:49.430 align:middle line:90%
that has the same cost.

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:50.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes, question?

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:54.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's, like, not
necessarily true that a plus--

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:57.350 align:middle line:84%
that alpha plus beta
is greater than 1.

00:38:57.350 --> 00:39:00.765 align:middle line:84%
So are there any constraints
that would make alpha plus beta

00:39:00.765 --> 00:39:01.390 align:middle line:90%
greater than 1?

00:39:01.390 --> 00:39:03.590 align:middle line:84%
Or in the real
world, is it possible

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:06.270 align:middle line:84%
that alpha plus
beta is less than 1?

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:08.756 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: I see.

00:39:08.756 --> 00:39:18.050 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, probably-- no, I think
it could go either way.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:21.510 align:middle line:84%
So I guess one
example would be--

00:39:21.510 --> 00:39:23.230 align:middle line:84%
let's think of
a-- there might be

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:29.150 align:middle line:84%
a very cheap surgery that really
improves my quality of life.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:32.610 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, the value
that I get out of that surgery,

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.710 align:middle line:84%
my willingness to pay
might be much, much higher

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:36.790 align:middle line:84%
than the cost of
producing that surgery.

00:39:36.790 --> 00:39:38.730 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, it
might go the other way.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:41.100 align:middle line:84%
There could be a really
expensive new treatment that

00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:42.665 align:middle line:84%
doesn't really help
me out very much,

00:39:42.665 --> 00:39:45.040 align:middle line:84%
and it may be hugely expensive
to provide this treatment,

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:46.580 align:middle line:84%
but the utility gain
and my willingness

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:48.640 align:middle line:84%
to pay for that treatment
might actually be quite low.

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:50.140 align:middle line:84%
So I think in general
you could have

00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:53.135 align:middle line:84%
either ordering between v and
c depending on the context

00:39:53.135 --> 00:39:53.760 align:middle line:90%
that you're in.

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:55.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this is a
very stylized model.

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:59.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying-- if we wanted
to really directly conduct

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:01.680 align:middle line:84%
health care policy, we'd have
to use a more complex model,

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:03.160 align:middle line:84%
but just to convey the
ideas, I want to do that.

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:04.535 align:middle line:84%
But yes, I think
there could be--

00:40:04.535 --> 00:40:07.922 align:middle line:84%
and in fact, this is an issue,
when people have full insurance

00:40:07.922 --> 00:40:09.880 align:middle line:84%
and they don't have to
pay to go to the doctor,

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:12.420 align:middle line:84%
they sometimes go to the
doctor and get procedures

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:14.240 align:middle line:84%
that are extremely
expensive to provide,

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:15.860 align:middle line:84%
but don't actually help them
that much because they don't

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:17.900 align:middle line:84%
have to pay, and that's
one kind of issue

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:19.600 align:middle line:90%
with insurance markets, yeah.

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:20.100 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:22.580 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions on this?

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:26.340 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now let's try
to work through--

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:28.500 align:middle line:84%
an analogous problem
is over there.

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:31.760 align:middle line:84%
We still have the seller
choosing to post a price,

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:33.020 align:middle line:90%
but now what's different?

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:36.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's only going to be
the high-value people who

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:37.732 align:middle line:90%
buy at a given price.

00:40:37.732 --> 00:40:39.190 align:middle line:84%
But what's the key
difference here?

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:40.450 align:middle line:84%
What's going to be true
about the people who

00:40:40.450 --> 00:40:41.617 align:middle line:90%
choose to buy the insurance?

00:40:41.617 --> 00:40:43.010 align:middle line:84%
If I set a price,
only the people

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.135 align:middle line:84%
who value it more than that
price are going to buy.

00:40:45.135 --> 00:40:48.010 align:middle line:84%
What's going to be true
about those people?

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:49.650 align:middle line:90%
Who's going to buy my insurance?

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:50.407 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:40:50.407 --> 00:40:52.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The people that
have high costs as well.

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:53.950 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The people that
also have high costs.

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:56.200 align:middle line:84%
And this is the fundamental
idea of adverse selection.

00:40:56.200 --> 00:40:57.730 align:middle line:84%
If I go out into
the market and I

00:40:57.730 --> 00:40:59.647 align:middle line:84%
say this is the price
I'm going to offer,

00:40:59.647 --> 00:41:01.730 align:middle line:84%
then the people who are
going to buy the insurance

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:04.230 align:middle line:84%
are going to be the people who
are most expensive to insure,

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:05.830 align:middle line:90%
and that can create problems.

00:41:05.830 --> 00:41:09.295 align:middle line:84%
My favorite example of adverse
selection is in the 1990s--

00:41:09.295 --> 00:41:10.670 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
I told you this--

00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:11.650 align:middle line:84%
I tell this story
every year, sorry,

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:12.790 align:middle line:90%
I forget which year I told it.

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:14.915 align:middle line:84%
In the 1990s, United or
Delta, one of the airlines,

00:41:14.915 --> 00:41:17.210 align:middle line:84%
offered an unlimited flying
program where you could fly

00:41:17.210 --> 00:41:18.570 align:middle line:90%
forever.

00:41:18.570 --> 00:41:22.250 align:middle line:84%
And it cost, I think, like
$200,000 in today's terms.

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:24.830 align:middle line:84%
And Delta thought, well, most
people don't fly that much.

00:41:24.830 --> 00:41:26.498 align:middle line:90%
$200,000, that's a lot.

00:41:26.498 --> 00:41:28.290 align:middle line:84%
It was a disastrous
program for the airline

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:30.123 align:middle line:84%
because there's some
really weird people who

00:41:30.123 --> 00:41:30.930 align:middle line:90%
just like to fly.

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:32.690 align:middle line:84%
And one guy just
took the program

00:41:32.690 --> 00:41:34.570 align:middle line:84%
and just flew first class around
the world every single day

00:41:34.570 --> 00:41:35.770 align:middle line:90%
for the rest of his life.

00:41:35.770 --> 00:41:37.395 align:middle line:84%
That's an example of
adverse selection.

00:41:37.395 --> 00:41:39.000 align:middle line:84%
When you post a
price, the person who

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:40.622 align:middle line:84%
buys it is not
the average flyer,

00:41:40.622 --> 00:41:42.580 align:middle line:84%
it's the person who really
values flying a lot.

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:44.920 align:middle line:84%
And this was a person who
was extremely costly to give

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:46.220 align:middle line:90%
an unlimited flying license to.

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:48.780 align:middle line:84%
For me, probably 200K, the
airline would make money on me.

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:50.960 align:middle line:84%
Of course, my behavior
would change once I got it.

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:52.820 align:middle line:84%
But in this guy's case,
they lost a lot of money,

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:54.612 align:middle line:84%
and they ended up going
to court and trying

00:41:54.612 --> 00:41:56.940 align:middle line:84%
to nullify the contract, and
it was this big, big mess.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:57.440 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:41:57.440 --> 00:41:59.680 align:middle line:84%
I guess another adverse
selection effect

00:41:59.680 --> 00:42:01.580 align:middle line:90%
might be health insurance.

00:42:01.580 --> 00:42:06.940 align:middle line:84%
Another might be in
financial markets.

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:13.640 align:middle line:90%


00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:16.418 align:middle line:84%
If I'm selling a
mortgage-backed security

00:42:16.418 --> 00:42:17.960 align:middle line:84%
and I don't know
much about the value

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:19.480 align:middle line:84%
of this mortgage-backed
security and someone's

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:21.120 align:middle line:84%
willing to buy it from
me, well, the fact

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:22.440 align:middle line:84%
that they're willing
to buy it probably

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:24.260 align:middle line:84%
means it's very valuable and
I'm not getting a good price.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:26.380 align:middle line:84%
If they're not willing to buy
it, then it goes the other way.

00:42:26.380 --> 00:42:27.480 align:middle line:84%
So there's always the
problem, you always

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:29.905 align:middle line:84%
have to think, why is this
person willing to buy from me?

00:42:29.905 --> 00:42:31.780 align:middle line:84%
And often, if they're
willing to buy from me,

00:42:31.780 --> 00:42:33.100 align:middle line:84%
it might mean I don't
want to sell it to them,

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:35.240 align:middle line:84%
and this is the fundamental
issue of adverse selection.

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:37.490 align:middle line:84%
If someone's willing to buy
an unlimited flight ticket

00:42:37.490 --> 00:42:40.740 align:middle line:84%
for 200K, maybe it's not a
good idea to sell it to them.

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:44.260 align:middle line:84%
OK, but let's now get
into the math here.

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:48.340 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose I
offer a price of p.

00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:57.300 align:middle line:84%
So the seller offers a price p,
then the buyer buys if and only

00:42:57.300 --> 00:42:59.660 align:middle line:84%
if-- well, as before,
their value is

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:02.100 align:middle line:90%
greater than or equal to p.

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:05.880 align:middle line:84%
And now, instead of saying
p is between 0 and 1,

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:10.140 align:middle line:84%
let's say p is between
alpha and alpha plus beta.

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:12.163 align:middle line:84%
Because, again, if I
charge a price below alpha,

00:43:12.163 --> 00:43:14.580 align:middle line:84%
no one's going to buy, and if
I charge it above alpha plus

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:17.180 align:middle line:84%
beta-- sorry, below alpha,
everyone's going to buy,

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:19.600 align:middle line:84%
and above alpha plus beta,
no one's going to buy,

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:22.260 align:middle line:84%
so we can just focus
on this range--

00:43:22.260 --> 00:43:24.340 align:middle line:90%
this range of value space.

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:26.900 align:middle line:84%
So c buys-- or the
buyer buys if and only

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:28.420 align:middle line:84%
if v is greater
than or equal to p.

00:43:28.420 --> 00:43:29.545 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's write that out.

00:43:29.545 --> 00:43:35.410 align:middle line:84%
That means alpha plus beta c
is greater than or equal to p.

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:37.790 align:middle line:84%
Here, I've just written
out what v means.

00:43:37.790 --> 00:43:40.170 align:middle line:84%
And now let's
solve for c, and we

00:43:40.170 --> 00:43:49.770 align:middle line:84%
get c is greater than or
equal to p minus alpha over B.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:51.850 align:middle line:90%
So now we can compute--

00:43:51.850 --> 00:43:54.070 align:middle line:84%
let's actually go over
to this board here.

00:43:54.070 --> 00:44:04.730 align:middle line:90%


00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:08.810 align:middle line:84%
Let's compute pi
of p, which is what

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:12.850 align:middle line:84%
is the seller's expected profit
if they post a price of p?

00:44:12.850 --> 00:44:19.730 align:middle line:84%
Now over here, we just wrote p
minus c times the probability

00:44:19.730 --> 00:44:21.770 align:middle line:84%
that v is greater
than or equal to p.

00:44:21.770 --> 00:44:23.230 align:middle line:90%
This part stays the same.

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:25.130 align:middle line:84%
This is the probability
they buy, but now we

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:27.250 align:middle line:90%
have to adjust p minus c.

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:31.160 align:middle line:84%
Because now, there's not one c,
there's a population of people.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:33.040 align:middle line:84%
And the c that we
care about is not

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:34.900 align:middle line:84%
the average sea
in the population,

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:38.160 align:middle line:84%
but it's the average c
among the people who buy.

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:41.300 align:middle line:84%
So we crucially need a
conditional expectation.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:44.160 align:middle line:90%


00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:46.040 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll write it this way.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:50.888 align:middle line:84%
p minus e of c, given
that someone buys,

00:44:50.888 --> 00:44:52.680 align:middle line:84%
and that means given
that c is greater than

00:44:52.680 --> 00:44:59.120 align:middle line:84%
or equal to p minus
alpha over B, over beta.

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:02.997 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to multiply
that by the probability

00:45:02.997 --> 00:45:04.580 align:middle line:84%
that v is greater
than or equal to p--

00:45:04.580 --> 00:45:06.080 align:middle line:84%
or maybe I'll write
it the same way.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:10.460 align:middle line:84%
I'll write this as c just to
make the math a bit easier.

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:15.520 align:middle line:90%


00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:18.280 align:middle line:84%
And now we can see this
pretty clearly in our graph.

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:24.080 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I post
a price of p here.

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:25.720 align:middle line:90%
Well, who's going to buy?

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:32.220 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be people along
this segment of society.

00:45:32.220 --> 00:45:35.420 align:middle line:84%
These are going to be the
sickest people, the people who

00:45:35.420 --> 00:45:37.060 align:middle line:90%
value insurance the most.

00:45:37.060 --> 00:45:41.940 align:middle line:84%
And a naive mistake that I think
historically some companies made

00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:44.580 align:middle line:84%
is they did this calculation
just taking the expected cost

00:45:44.580 --> 00:45:46.500 align:middle line:90%
in the population.

00:45:46.500 --> 00:45:48.740 align:middle line:84%
Not recognizing that,
well, I shouldn't

00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:51.140 align:middle line:84%
take the expectation
over the entire line,

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:52.750 align:middle line:84%
I need to only take
the expectation

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:55.000 align:middle line:84%
among the people who are
actually buying my insurance.

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:57.788 align:middle line:84%
Or in the airline example,
only among the people--

00:45:57.788 --> 00:45:59.580 align:middle line:84%
the flying frequency
of people who actually

00:45:59.580 --> 00:46:02.820 align:middle line:90%
buy an unlimited flying ticket.

00:46:02.820 --> 00:46:06.140 align:middle line:84%
So now let's try to
compute this expectation.

00:46:06.140 --> 00:46:10.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out, c is
uniformly distributed.

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:17.740 align:middle line:84%
Down here, this is actually p
minus alpha over B, over beta.

00:46:17.740 --> 00:46:19.380 align:middle line:90%
So I'm only focusing--

00:46:19.380 --> 00:46:22.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm only actually selling to
people who lie in this range.

00:46:22.700 --> 00:46:24.900 align:middle line:84%
And because c is
uniformly distributed,

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:26.930 align:middle line:84%
the average cost among
people in this range

00:46:26.930 --> 00:46:29.290 align:middle line:84%
is just going to be the
midpoint of this range.

00:46:29.290 --> 00:46:33.330 align:middle line:90%
So this is going to be p minus--

00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:43.490 align:middle line:84%
well, the midpoint of this is
going to be 1/2 times 1 plus p

00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:46.970 align:middle line:90%
minus alpha over beta.

00:46:46.970 --> 00:46:49.990 align:middle line:84%
So it's still price
minus roughly cost,

00:46:49.990 --> 00:46:52.530 align:middle line:84%
but now it's expected cost
conditional on buying,

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:55.010 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm going to need
to put this probability.

00:46:55.010 --> 00:46:58.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, this probability
is just the length--

00:46:58.770 --> 00:47:03.050 align:middle line:84%
or the length of this segment,
which is 1 minus p minus

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:03.990 align:middle line:90%
alpha over beta.

00:47:03.990 --> 00:47:14.730 align:middle line:90%


00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:18.010 align:middle line:90%
And let's-- I think it's--

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:19.690 align:middle line:84%
we're interested
in p, so let's try

00:47:19.690 --> 00:47:22.330 align:middle line:84%
to do a little algebra
to get p by itself here.

00:47:22.330 --> 00:47:27.200 align:middle line:84%
So in this, we have p here, and
we have minus 1/2 times 1 over

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:27.920 align:middle line:90%
beta.

00:47:27.920 --> 00:47:41.582 align:middle line:84%
So we get p times 1 minus 1 over
2 beta minus 1/2 times alpha

00:47:41.582 --> 00:47:43.060 align:middle line:90%
over beta.

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:55.200 align:middle line:90%


00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:59.720 align:middle line:84%
So now let's analyze
what's going on here.

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:02.940 align:middle line:84%
This is the purchase
probability.

00:48:02.940 --> 00:48:08.960 align:middle line:90%


00:48:08.960 --> 00:48:18.280 align:middle line:84%
And this is the
profit per purchase.

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:21.320 align:middle line:84%
Now, as usual, the
purchase probability

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:23.020 align:middle line:90%
is decreasing in the price.

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:24.420 align:middle line:84%
This is the effect
we always see.

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:27.420 align:middle line:84%
When I'm a firm and I charge a
higher price, fewer people buy.

00:48:27.420 --> 00:48:29.020 align:middle line:90%
That's very intuitive.

00:48:29.020 --> 00:48:31.300 align:middle line:90%
The new effect is here.

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:33.740 align:middle line:84%
This is saying, what
profit do I make

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:36.420 align:middle line:90%
per person who buys from me?

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:40.620 align:middle line:84%
And notice that the coefficient
on p before, the coefficient

00:48:40.620 --> 00:48:43.820 align:middle line:84%
on p was 1 When I charged
a price, when I increased

00:48:43.820 --> 00:48:46.620 align:middle line:84%
the price by $1, every time
someone bought from me,

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:49.460 align:middle line:84%
the amount of revenue I
generated-- the profit that I

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:51.580 align:middle line:84%
got from that person
increased by $1.

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:54.400 align:middle line:84%
But now, it doesn't
increase by $1.

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:56.638 align:middle line:84%
It increases by $1
minus 1 over 2 beta.

00:48:56.638 --> 00:48:57.680 align:middle line:90%
So let's understand this.

00:48:57.680 --> 00:49:00.220 align:middle line:90%
I raise my price by $1.

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:02.640 align:middle line:84%
I don't make a more per
person I'm selling to,

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:04.797 align:middle line:84%
I only make one minus
1 over 2 beta more,

00:49:04.797 --> 00:49:05.880 align:middle line:90%
which is strictly smaller.

00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:07.420 align:middle line:90%
I don't get the full dollar.

00:49:07.420 --> 00:49:08.280 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:10.600 align:middle line:90%
So why is this less than 1?

00:49:10.600 --> 00:49:13.980 align:middle line:90%


00:49:13.980 --> 00:49:14.692 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:49:14.692 --> 00:49:16.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because as
you raise the price,

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:18.840 align:middle line:84%
the people who will be buying
it get more value out of it,

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:20.510 align:middle line:84%
so they'll cost
more [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:20.510 --> 00:49:21.510 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:23.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is reflecting
two effects.

00:49:23.690 --> 00:49:25.930 align:middle line:84%
The first effect is, yes,
when I raise my price

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:29.550 align:middle line:84%
by $1, my revenue per
patient increases by $1.

00:49:29.550 --> 00:49:30.970 align:middle line:90%
That always happens.

00:49:30.970 --> 00:49:33.630 align:middle line:84%
But the second effect is
when I raise my price by $1,

00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:37.370 align:middle line:84%
I change the composition of
the people who buy from me.

00:49:37.370 --> 00:49:41.330 align:middle line:84%
I shift the people who buy from
me to a less healthy subset

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:45.375 align:middle line:84%
of people, and on average, those
people are costlier to serve.

00:49:45.375 --> 00:49:47.250 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, this is
showing they're costlier

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:48.950 align:middle line:90%
by exactly 1 over 2 beta.

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:51.730 align:middle line:90%


00:49:51.730 --> 00:49:53.290 align:middle line:90%
Where does that come from?

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:57.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can see here, these are
the segment of people I serve.

00:49:57.010 --> 00:50:00.690 align:middle line:84%
When I raise my price by $1,
I increase the left endpoint

00:50:00.690 --> 00:50:03.330 align:middle line:84%
of this segment by 1 over
beta, and therefore, I

00:50:03.330 --> 00:50:05.950 align:middle line:84%
increase the average of this
segment by 1 over 2 beta.

00:50:05.950 --> 00:50:07.970 align:middle line:90%
So this is exactly the--

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:09.850 align:middle line:84%
1 over 2 beta is
the marginal effect

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:13.170 align:middle line:84%
of my price on the average
cost among the people

00:50:13.170 --> 00:50:16.130 align:middle line:90%
who buy from me.

00:50:16.130 --> 00:50:19.320 align:middle line:84%
More precisely, it's the
derivative of this expression

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:20.260 align:middle line:90%
with respect to p.

00:50:20.260 --> 00:50:24.000 align:middle line:90%


00:50:24.000 --> 00:50:28.280 align:middle line:90%
And we can see--

00:50:28.280 --> 00:50:30.800 align:middle line:84%
let's look at different
cases of beta.

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So what if beta is large
and what if beta is small?

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:42.600 align:middle line:84%
So if beta is very
large, adverse selection

00:50:42.600 --> 00:50:45.120 align:middle line:90%
is not much of a problem.

00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:53.480 align:middle line:84%
If beta is very large, this
curve is very, very steep.

00:50:53.480 --> 00:50:57.400 align:middle line:84%
And if beta is large,
it says that there's

00:50:57.400 --> 00:51:02.440 align:middle line:84%
not a huge effect on the average
cost when I change the price.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:04.160 align:middle line:84%
Because if beta
is large, it says

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:09.062 align:middle line:84%
that c doesn't change
much with v. Another way--

00:51:09.062 --> 00:51:10.520 align:middle line:84%
I think the easiest
way to see this

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:13.680 align:middle line:84%
is to solve this expression
for c as we've done.

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:23.620 align:middle line:84%
And so note, c equals
v minus alpha over B.

00:51:23.620 --> 00:51:25.680 align:middle line:84%
So when I change the
price by a little bit,

00:51:25.680 --> 00:51:28.300 align:middle line:84%
I'm changing by a little
bit the value of people

00:51:28.300 --> 00:51:29.360 align:middle line:90%
who are buying from me.

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:32.117 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, if
someone's value changes a bit,

00:51:32.117 --> 00:51:34.200 align:middle line:84%
someone's value of buying
for me changes by a bit,

00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:36.460 align:middle line:84%
how much does my cost of
providing to them change?

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:39.460 align:middle line:84%
And we see, it's exactly
proportional to 1 over beta.

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:41.380 align:middle line:84%
So when beta is
really, really large,

00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:43.500 align:middle line:84%
adverse selection is
not much of a concern.

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:45.000 align:middle line:90%
So little adverse selection.

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:50.860 align:middle line:90%


00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:53.380 align:middle line:84%
When beta grows
small, then people

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:55.540 align:middle line:84%
who value the item
just a little bit more

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:58.420 align:middle line:90%
are extremely costly to insure.

00:51:58.420 --> 00:52:02.600 align:middle line:84%
So we have severe
adverse selection.

00:52:02.600 --> 00:52:08.260 align:middle line:90%


00:52:08.260 --> 00:52:15.530 align:middle line:84%
And let's look at the case
where beta is greater than 1/2.

00:52:15.530 --> 00:52:17.330 align:middle line:90%
Or, sorry, less than 1/2.

00:52:17.330 --> 00:52:20.810 align:middle line:90%
So this is the extreme case.

00:52:20.810 --> 00:52:22.590 align:middle line:84%
What happens if beta
is less than 1/2?

00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:24.030 align:middle line:84%
What happens to
this coefficient?

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:27.470 align:middle line:90%


00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:27.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:52:27.970 --> 00:52:28.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's negative?

00:52:28.970 --> 00:52:30.090 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: It's negative.

00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:31.048 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:52:31.048 --> 00:52:34.690 align:middle line:84%
I raise prices and I
actually earn less per person

00:52:34.690 --> 00:52:36.110 align:middle line:90%
than I did with the lower price.

00:52:36.110 --> 00:52:39.410 align:middle line:90%


00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:43.050 align:middle line:84%
What it says is if
beta is less than 1/2,

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:46.890 align:middle line:84%
the shift in the composition of
people who buy from me more than

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:49.410 align:middle line:84%
outweighs the fact that I'm
charging a higher price.

00:52:49.410 --> 00:52:53.010 align:middle line:84%
And I'm actually earning
less per patient I serve.

00:52:53.010 --> 00:52:55.210 align:middle line:84%
And what that
means-- maybe we'll

00:52:55.210 --> 00:52:59.470 align:middle line:90%
call this market breakdown.

00:52:59.470 --> 00:53:02.530 align:middle line:90%


00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:03.050 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:53:03.050 --> 00:53:08.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, if beta is less than 1/2
then this is decreasing in p,

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:11.330 align:middle line:84%
and this is also
decreasing in p.

00:53:11.330 --> 00:53:13.320 align:middle line:84%
So that shows my
profit is certainly

00:53:13.320 --> 00:53:21.180 align:middle line:84%
decreasing in p over the range
alpha to alpha plus beta.

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:24.640 align:middle line:90%


00:53:24.640 --> 00:53:28.040 align:middle line:84%
So when I say
market breakdown, I

00:53:28.040 --> 00:53:30.020 align:middle line:90%
guess the only possibility is--

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:37.040 align:middle line:90%


00:53:37.040 --> 00:53:40.300 align:middle line:84%
maybe what I mean is
screening is not possible.

00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:43.840 align:middle line:90%


00:53:43.840 --> 00:53:46.920 align:middle line:84%
Because the optimal
price is either p

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:48.500 align:middle line:90%
equals alpha or no sale.

00:53:48.500 --> 00:53:55.120 align:middle line:90%


00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:59.640 align:middle line:84%
If my profit is decreasing in
p, the only possible maximum

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.520 align:middle line:84%
of this expression would
be at p equals alpha

00:54:01.520 --> 00:54:02.660 align:middle line:90%
at the left endpoint.

00:54:02.660 --> 00:54:04.840 align:middle line:84%
So I either have to serve
everyone in the market

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:05.850 align:middle line:90%
or serve no one.

00:54:05.850 --> 00:54:08.100 align:middle line:84%
And it may be optimal actually
for me to serve no one.

00:54:08.100 --> 00:54:09.780 align:middle line:84%
But what it says is there
can't be a screening effect.

00:54:09.780 --> 00:54:12.405 align:middle line:84%
I can't charge an intermediate
price and get some people to buy

00:54:12.405 --> 00:54:13.680 align:middle line:90%
and some people not to buy.

00:54:13.680 --> 00:54:16.140 align:middle line:84%
The only possibility is I
set the price low enough

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:22.703 align:middle line:84%
that everyone buys from me
or I just don't sell at all.

00:54:22.703 --> 00:54:24.620 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can interpret
everyone buying from me

00:54:24.620 --> 00:54:26.580 align:middle line:90%
as kind of universal coverage.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:28.660 align:middle line:84%
We can't have a
premium insurance plan

00:54:28.660 --> 00:54:30.023 align:middle line:90%
that only certain people buy.

00:54:30.023 --> 00:54:31.940 align:middle line:84%
That can never be optimal
if adverse selection

00:54:31.940 --> 00:54:32.648 align:middle line:90%
is severe enough.

00:54:32.648 --> 00:54:33.340 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:54:33.340 --> 00:54:36.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so if you sell to
everyone at a price of alpha,

00:54:36.800 --> 00:54:38.480 align:middle line:90%
is your profit supposed to be 0?

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:39.900 align:middle line:90%
Is that what the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:42.150 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It's going to
depend on the values of alpha.

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:43.700 align:middle line:90%
So in some cases--

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:45.220 align:middle line:84%
so if you set the
price to alpha,

00:54:45.220 --> 00:54:46.580 align:middle line:90%
you're going to get some number.

00:54:46.580 --> 00:54:49.260 align:middle line:84%
If that number is-- if your
profit is greater than 0,

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:50.160 align:middle line:90%
that's the optimum.

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:52.678 align:middle line:84%
If it's less than 0, you're
better off selling to no one.

00:54:52.678 --> 00:54:55.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, so your point is
just that if beta is less than

00:54:55.220 --> 00:54:59.297 align:middle line:84%
1/2, then the only option--
like, you have to solve--

00:54:59.297 --> 00:54:59.880 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes.

00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:01.120 align:middle line:90%
There's no interior solution.

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:03.078 align:middle line:84%
Either I sell to everyone
or I sell to no one.

00:55:03.078 --> 00:55:04.620 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we could go
more into details

00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:06.720 align:middle line:84%
depending on the details
of alpha and beta,

00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:10.930 align:middle line:84%
but basically, we
have a function,

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:15.250 align:middle line:90%
and it's going like this.

00:55:15.250 --> 00:55:22.982 align:middle line:84%
So its maximum is at
alpha equals 0, yeah.

00:55:22.982 --> 00:55:24.690 align:middle line:84%
We have to be careful
if sometimes things

00:55:24.690 --> 00:55:25.390 align:middle line:90%
can be negative.

00:55:25.390 --> 00:55:26.927 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah, when I say it's--

00:55:26.927 --> 00:55:28.010 align:middle line:90%
OK, we have to be careful.

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:30.723 align:middle line:84%
When we say it's decreasing
in beta, that's not true

00:55:30.723 --> 00:55:32.390 align:middle line:84%
if one of these
expressions is negative,

00:55:32.390 --> 00:55:34.098 align:middle line:84%
so let me make the
more precise statement

00:55:34.098 --> 00:55:37.710 align:middle line:84%
that the solution occurs
either at alpha or beta--

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:38.710 align:middle line:90%
at alpha plus beta.

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:40.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so why
does it make sense

00:55:40.850 --> 00:55:44.250 align:middle line:90%
that you would sell to everyone?

00:55:44.250 --> 00:55:46.650 align:middle line:84%
Like, what about
beta makes it so

00:55:46.650 --> 00:55:50.557 align:middle line:84%
that you could potentially make
money from selling everyone?

00:55:50.557 --> 00:55:52.890 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So whether it's
profitable to sell to everyone

00:55:52.890 --> 00:55:54.690 align:middle line:84%
is really going to
depend on alpha.

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:57.170 align:middle line:84%
What beta is controlling
is what happens

00:55:57.170 --> 00:55:59.090 align:middle line:90%
when I slightly raise my price.

00:55:59.090 --> 00:56:01.530 align:middle line:84%
So when beta is
very, very small,

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:04.410 align:middle line:84%
it says adverse selection is
so severe that I would never

00:56:04.410 --> 00:56:06.840 align:middle line:84%
want to sell to only some
people in the population

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:08.960 align:middle line:84%
because by raising my
price a little bit,

00:56:08.960 --> 00:56:11.160 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually earning
less per person

00:56:11.160 --> 00:56:13.280 align:middle line:84%
because those people
are so much sicker.

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:15.095 align:middle line:84%
So when that effect
is really strong,

00:56:15.095 --> 00:56:17.220 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to be vulnerable
to adverse selection.

00:56:17.220 --> 00:56:19.640 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to sell to the
subset of the population who

00:56:19.640 --> 00:56:20.420 align:middle line:90%
value it most.

00:56:20.420 --> 00:56:22.640 align:middle line:84%
I either want to sell to
everyone or sell to no one.

00:56:22.640 --> 00:56:24.000 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be
controlled by alpha.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:25.360 align:middle line:90%
Alpha could be enormous.

00:56:25.360 --> 00:56:28.060 align:middle line:84%
If alpha is a million
and c is pretty small,

00:56:28.060 --> 00:56:30.800 align:middle line:84%
then I certainly want
to sell to everyone.

00:56:30.800 --> 00:56:32.510 align:middle line:84%
Alpha is kind of
about a level effect.

00:56:32.510 --> 00:56:34.260 align:middle line:84%
It's like, is this a
good market to be in?

00:56:34.260 --> 00:56:35.840 align:middle line:84%
Do I want to sell
to people or not?

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:38.000 align:middle line:84%
Beta is a marginal
effect, which is

00:56:38.000 --> 00:56:40.240 align:middle line:84%
controlling what
happens to my profit

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:42.080 align:middle line:90%
as I slightly change my price.

00:56:42.080 --> 00:56:44.480 align:middle line:84%
And it's saying, if
beta is small enough,

00:56:44.480 --> 00:56:47.760 align:middle line:84%
the adverse selection
effect is so strong

00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:49.807 align:middle line:84%
that I never want
to slightly shrink

00:56:49.807 --> 00:56:51.640 align:middle line:84%
the set of people I
sell to because the cost

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:53.140 align:middle line:84%
among those people is
going to be so much higher.

00:56:53.140 --> 00:56:53.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes, again.

00:56:53.720 --> 00:56:55.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking
about real-world examples

00:56:55.720 --> 00:56:57.100 align:middle line:84%
where you have
insurance markets,

00:56:57.100 --> 00:56:58.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm thinking like
disaster insurance

00:56:58.860 --> 00:57:01.880 align:middle line:84%
where if you live in a certain
area, you have to buy insurance,

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:07.670 align:middle line:84%
or if you have health care
given by your employer.

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:10.830 align:middle line:84%
Would that be a
case where you just

00:57:10.830 --> 00:57:14.530 align:middle line:84%
end up charging alpha because
of the policy intervention?

00:57:14.530 --> 00:57:17.415 align:middle line:84%
Like the insurance company
charges alpha for everyone.

00:57:17.415 --> 00:57:19.790 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so I guess a
separate issue that comes up

00:57:19.790 --> 00:57:22.683 align:middle line:84%
with disaster insurance is that
often, disasters are correlated.

00:57:22.683 --> 00:57:24.350 align:middle line:84%
So you have a heart
attack, someone else

00:57:24.350 --> 00:57:25.730 align:middle line:84%
is more likely to
have a heart attack.

00:57:25.730 --> 00:57:27.030 align:middle line:84%
If you get hit by a
hurricane, everyone's

00:57:27.030 --> 00:57:28.210 align:middle line:90%
often hit by a hurricane.

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:30.710 align:middle line:84%
So often there's an issue
that these disasters

00:57:30.710 --> 00:57:32.350 align:middle line:90%
are too big to insure.

00:57:32.350 --> 00:57:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So often, the state steps in
and provides the insurance

00:57:34.710 --> 00:57:36.252 align:middle line:84%
because no private
company can do it,

00:57:36.252 --> 00:57:40.390 align:middle line:84%
and then the state puts
in these regulations.

00:57:40.390 --> 00:57:44.550 align:middle line:84%
I think really, what's going
on in those cases-- there's

00:57:44.550 --> 00:57:47.070 align:middle line:84%
a lot going on with
disaster insurance.

00:57:47.070 --> 00:57:52.910 align:middle line:84%
In this case, here,
I think normally,

00:57:52.910 --> 00:57:54.483 align:middle line:84%
with reasonable
parameter values--

00:57:54.483 --> 00:57:56.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this is a
very simplified model.

00:57:56.150 --> 00:57:57.530 align:middle line:84%
With reasonable
parameter values,

00:57:57.530 --> 00:58:00.110 align:middle line:84%
when adverse selection is bad,
the solution is probably going

00:58:00.110 --> 00:58:02.410 align:middle line:84%
to be to sell to no one
because you're going to have--

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:06.015 align:middle line:84%
if you have any really healthy
people who are willing to pay

00:58:06.015 --> 00:58:07.390 align:middle line:84%
a very low amount
for insurance--

00:58:07.390 --> 00:58:08.350 align:middle line:90%
I mean, think about it.

00:58:08.350 --> 00:58:11.610 align:middle line:84%
Let's say-- if I'm
supposed to insure everyone

00:58:11.610 --> 00:58:13.250 align:middle line:84%
in the population,
but I can only

00:58:13.250 --> 00:58:14.917 align:middle line:84%
charge a price that
makes the healthiest

00:58:14.917 --> 00:58:17.050 align:middle line:84%
people willing to buy,
then that's probably not

00:58:17.050 --> 00:58:18.300 align:middle line:90%
going to be profitable for me.

00:58:18.300 --> 00:58:21.290 align:middle line:84%
So I think what I would say
is, in realistic settings,

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:23.050 align:middle line:84%
the alpha where
markets break down,

00:58:23.050 --> 00:58:25.450 align:middle line:84%
the alpha is going to be small
enough so that there's not

00:58:25.450 --> 00:58:28.270 align:middle line:84%
going to be a sustainable
private insurance market.

00:58:28.270 --> 00:58:31.228 align:middle line:84%
Of course, there's a separate
issue here that we haven't--

00:58:31.228 --> 00:58:32.770 align:middle line:84%
there's a separate
question, which is

00:58:32.770 --> 00:58:34.290 align:middle line:90%
how well can we monitor people?

00:58:34.290 --> 00:58:35.707 align:middle line:84%
So another thing
that could happen

00:58:35.707 --> 00:58:37.750 align:middle line:84%
is you might impose very
strict health standards.

00:58:37.750 --> 00:58:40.333 align:middle line:84%
You might say-- there might be
a functioning insurance market,

00:58:40.333 --> 00:58:41.850 align:middle line:90%
but only for people under 50.

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:44.392 align:middle line:84%
And then people over 50 are
just excluded from the market.

00:58:44.392 --> 00:58:46.850 align:middle line:84%
And that conditioning is not
present, is not captured here,

00:58:46.850 --> 00:58:48.970 align:middle line:84%
or at least the
interpretation of this would

00:58:48.970 --> 00:58:51.850 align:middle line:84%
have to be after we conduct
medical tests and after we

00:58:51.850 --> 00:58:55.490 align:middle line:84%
condition, and then beta
might become bigger, yeah.

00:58:55.490 --> 00:58:58.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I think the--

00:58:58.050 --> 00:58:59.470 align:middle line:84%
yeah I think the
insurance market,

00:58:59.470 --> 00:59:00.980 align:middle line:84%
the disaster insurance
is interesting,

00:59:00.980 --> 00:59:02.680 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's really a
separate issue because people

00:59:02.680 --> 00:59:04.400 align:middle line:84%
don't really have private
information about how

00:59:04.400 --> 00:59:05.840 align:middle line:90%
likely their home is to flood.

00:59:05.840 --> 00:59:07.960 align:middle line:84%
I mean, maybe if the government
has to use really blanket

00:59:07.960 --> 00:59:09.900 align:middle line:84%
policies and they're
not fine-grained enough,

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:11.380 align:middle line:84%
but at least in
the private market,

00:59:11.380 --> 00:59:13.588 align:middle line:84%
I think that's not really
an adverse selection issue,

00:59:13.588 --> 00:59:15.540 align:middle line:84%
it's really a
too-big-to-fail issue.

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:17.165 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but it's an
interesting question.

00:59:17.165 --> 00:59:19.280 align:middle line:84%
And, yeah, actually
one of my colleagues

00:59:19.280 --> 00:59:21.440 align:middle line:84%
is just writing a
paper about this

00:59:21.440 --> 00:59:23.960 align:middle line:84%
in California wildfire
insurance and looking

00:59:23.960 --> 00:59:25.220 align:middle line:90%
at how insurers handle this.

00:59:25.220 --> 00:59:27.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it's interesting.

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:27.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:27.800 --> 00:59:30.760 align:middle line:90%


00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:32.520 align:middle line:84%
We've talked a lot
about screening.

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:34.880 align:middle line:90%
Let's now talk about signaling.

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:36.480 align:middle line:84%
And this gets a
little messy, so we're

00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:38.600 align:middle line:84%
going to have to work with
an even simpler model.

00:59:38.600 --> 00:59:41.700 align:middle line:84%
And, yeah, we should have
time just to finish this off.

00:59:41.700 --> 00:59:56.120 align:middle line:90%


00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:59.810 align:middle line:84%
So now let's finally move
to the case of signaling.

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:02.590 align:middle line:90%


01:00:02.590 --> 01:00:04.330 align:middle line:84%
So we're still with
a seller and a buyer,

01:00:04.330 --> 01:00:06.372 align:middle line:84%
we're still in incomplete
information bargaining,

01:00:06.372 --> 01:00:10.750 align:middle line:84%
but now it's the seller who
has private information.

01:00:10.750 --> 01:00:16.110 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going
to say the seller has

01:00:16.110 --> 01:00:19.690 align:middle line:90%
private info about v and c.

01:00:19.690 --> 01:00:21.890 align:middle line:84%
So this is another
canonical application

01:00:21.890 --> 01:00:23.390 align:middle line:84%
where the seller--
we said insurance

01:00:23.390 --> 01:00:25.310 align:middle line:84%
is the canonical case
where the buyer has

01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:27.758 align:middle line:84%
private information that's
relevant to both v and c.

01:00:27.758 --> 01:00:29.550 align:middle line:84%
Can you think of a
setting where the seller

01:00:29.550 --> 01:00:33.270 align:middle line:84%
has private information
about v and c?

01:00:33.270 --> 01:00:38.130 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of a
classic story here.

01:00:38.130 --> 01:00:39.630 align:middle line:84%
Used cars, kind of
the classic case,

01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:40.880 align:middle line:90%
or more generally, used goods.

01:00:40.880 --> 01:00:44.208 align:middle line:90%


01:00:44.208 --> 01:00:45.750 align:middle line:84%
Here, I think it's
important to think

01:00:45.750 --> 01:00:48.470 align:middle line:84%
of c maybe not so much
as a cost, but more as

01:00:48.470 --> 01:00:50.252 align:middle line:84%
how much the seller
values the good.

01:00:50.252 --> 01:00:52.210 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is if I'm
going to buy a used car,

01:00:52.210 --> 01:00:54.150 align:middle line:84%
the seller of that
used car knows

01:00:54.150 --> 01:00:56.138 align:middle line:84%
whether it's making a
bad sound, whether it's

01:00:56.138 --> 01:00:58.430 align:middle line:84%
had a lot of accidents,
whether there's these problems.

01:00:58.430 --> 01:01:01.930 align:middle line:84%
Basically what they know
is the quality of the good.

01:01:01.930 --> 01:01:04.410 align:middle line:84%
And that quality,
unobserved quality,

01:01:04.410 --> 01:01:06.410 align:middle line:84%
is relevant both to how
much the buyer values it

01:01:06.410 --> 01:01:07.870 align:middle line:84%
and how much the
seller values it.

01:01:07.870 --> 01:01:09.910 align:middle line:84%
But because the seller has
had the good for a while,

01:01:09.910 --> 01:01:11.493 align:middle line:84%
they know a lot more
about it than you

01:01:11.493 --> 01:01:15.490 align:middle line:84%
as the buyer who come by and
just try to inspect your good.

01:01:15.490 --> 01:01:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And the classic case
would be used cars.

01:01:18.630 --> 01:01:24.570 align:middle line:84%
So there's a very famous paper
called "The Market for Lemons"

01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:25.570 align:middle line:90%
by Akerlof.

01:01:25.570 --> 01:01:28.010 align:middle line:84%
He won the Nobel
Prize for identifying

01:01:28.010 --> 01:01:31.010 align:middle line:84%
this issue of why used car
markets often don't work well,

01:01:31.010 --> 01:01:33.770 align:middle line:84%
and it's the same classic
thing that we said, going back

01:01:33.770 --> 01:01:35.770 align:middle line:84%
to the defendant
and the prosecutor,

01:01:35.770 --> 01:01:38.270 align:middle line:84%
if someone comes to you and
says, "I can't sell my used car,

01:01:38.270 --> 01:01:39.470 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to give
you a really good deal.

01:01:39.470 --> 01:01:40.550 align:middle line:84%
It's a really good deal,
it's really cheap."

01:01:40.550 --> 01:01:41.110 align:middle line:90%
What are you going to say?

01:01:41.110 --> 01:01:42.235 align:middle line:90%
"Wait, why is it so cheap?"

01:01:42.235 --> 01:01:44.030 align:middle line:84%
It's cheap because
it must be a lemon.

01:01:44.030 --> 01:01:46.613 align:middle line:84%
It must be-- that's another word
for a car that doesn't really

01:01:46.613 --> 01:01:47.290 align:middle line:90%
work.

01:01:47.290 --> 01:01:50.610 align:middle line:84%
And for that reason, these
markets can break down.

01:01:50.610 --> 01:01:53.030 align:middle line:84%
I think my financial example
was another good one.

01:01:53.030 --> 01:01:56.160 align:middle line:84%
So mortgage-backed
securities might

01:01:56.160 --> 01:01:57.680 align:middle line:84%
be another case
where it's not clear

01:01:57.680 --> 01:01:58.980 align:middle line:84%
which side has more
private information,

01:01:58.980 --> 01:02:00.900 align:middle line:84%
it depends who they
are, but in some cases,

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:02.160 align:middle line:84%
the holder of the
mortgage-backed security

01:02:02.160 --> 01:02:03.720 align:middle line:84%
may have private
information that's

01:02:03.720 --> 01:02:05.660 align:middle line:84%
relevant to the
value of this asset,

01:02:05.660 --> 01:02:08.520 align:middle line:84%
and both the buyer and the
seller care about that.

01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:11.080 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's look at a really
simplified model where we're

01:02:11.080 --> 01:02:19.040 align:middle line:84%
going to say that the good
has quality either L or H.

01:02:19.040 --> 01:02:21.660 align:middle line:84%
And here, these are not
numbers, these are just labels.

01:02:21.660 --> 01:02:23.910 align:middle line:84%
It's either low quality
or high quality.

01:02:23.910 --> 01:02:26.160 align:middle line:84%
But the quality the good is
relevant to both the buyer

01:02:26.160 --> 01:02:27.280 align:middle line:90%
and the seller.

01:02:27.280 --> 01:02:29.700 align:middle line:84%
So if it's a
low-quality good, then--

01:02:29.700 --> 01:02:38.920 align:middle line:90%


01:02:38.920 --> 01:02:40.760 align:middle line:90%
and, I don't know.

01:02:40.760 --> 01:02:43.000 align:middle line:84%
All these inequalities
may be confusing.

01:02:43.000 --> 01:02:48.960 align:middle line:84%
So if it's low quality,
then the seller's cost is cL

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:50.800 align:middle line:90%
and the buyer's value is vL.

01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:53.430 align:middle line:84%
If it's high quality,
the seller's cost is cH

01:02:53.430 --> 01:02:56.630 align:middle line:84%
and the buyer's value is vH, and
we have all these inequalities.

01:02:56.630 --> 01:02:58.990 align:middle line:84%
For the seller, a
low-quality item

01:02:58.990 --> 01:03:01.350 align:middle line:84%
is less costly than
a high-quality item,

01:03:01.350 --> 01:03:03.350 align:middle line:84%
but we also want
the seller's cost

01:03:03.350 --> 01:03:06.910 align:middle line:84%
to be lower than the buyer's
value for a low-quality item.

01:03:06.910 --> 01:03:10.270 align:middle line:84%
For a high-quality
item, the seller's cost

01:03:10.270 --> 01:03:11.850 align:middle line:84%
is smaller than
the buyer's value,

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:13.570 align:middle line:84%
so there are potential
gains from trade,

01:03:13.570 --> 01:03:15.230 align:middle line:84%
but again, the
buyer values it more

01:03:15.230 --> 01:03:17.510 align:middle line:84%
when it's high quality
than when it's low quality.

01:03:17.510 --> 01:03:20.870 align:middle line:84%
So there's a string
of inequalities here.

01:03:20.870 --> 01:03:23.890 align:middle line:84%
And now what we're going to
look at is price signaling.

01:03:23.890 --> 01:03:27.710 align:middle line:90%


01:03:27.710 --> 01:03:29.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to set a
price to the seller,

01:03:29.370 --> 01:03:31.110 align:middle line:84%
the buyer is going to
see that price and infer

01:03:31.110 --> 01:03:32.730 align:middle line:84%
something about the
value of the item,

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:35.230 align:middle line:84%
and then we want to try to solve
for an equilibrium of this.

01:03:35.230 --> 01:03:37.790 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to have to use
perfect Bayesian equilibrium

01:03:37.790 --> 01:03:38.930 align:middle line:90%
here to analyze this.

01:03:38.930 --> 01:03:50.150 align:middle line:90%


01:03:50.150 --> 01:03:54.010 align:middle line:84%
Now we need to specify
a prior distribution

01:03:54.010 --> 01:03:55.470 align:middle line:90%
about the quality of the good.

01:03:55.470 --> 01:03:57.330 align:middle line:84%
So let me say a
little bit more here.

01:03:57.330 --> 01:04:01.030 align:middle line:90%
The seller knows quality.

01:04:01.030 --> 01:04:03.492 align:middle line:90%


01:04:03.492 --> 01:04:04.950 align:middle line:84%
The buyer doesn't
know the quality,

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:06.450 align:middle line:84%
but they just know
the distribution

01:04:06.450 --> 01:04:08.130 align:middle line:90%
of quality in the population.

01:04:08.130 --> 01:04:09.930 align:middle line:90%
And we'll say that--

01:04:09.930 --> 01:04:14.290 align:middle line:84%
we'll put pi here
and 1 minus pi here.

01:04:14.290 --> 01:04:19.170 align:middle line:84%
So the prior distribution is
that fraction pi of things

01:04:19.170 --> 01:04:21.250 align:middle line:84%
are high quality, fraction
1 minus pi of things

01:04:21.250 --> 01:04:23.710 align:middle line:84%
are low quality, the seller
actually knows the quality,

01:04:23.710 --> 01:04:24.590 align:middle line:90%
the buyer does not.

01:04:24.590 --> 01:04:32.130 align:middle line:90%


01:04:32.130 --> 01:04:34.490 align:middle line:84%
And after observing
the price, the seller

01:04:34.490 --> 01:04:35.748 align:middle line:90%
is going to set a price.

01:04:35.748 --> 01:04:37.290 align:middle line:84%
The buyer sees the
price and is going

01:04:37.290 --> 01:04:39.498 align:middle line:84%
to update their beliefs
about the quality of the item

01:04:39.498 --> 01:04:40.450 align:middle line:90%
based upon the price.

01:04:40.450 --> 01:04:43.048 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to construct--

01:04:43.048 --> 01:04:45.090 align:middle line:84%
and I think you'll have
more practice doing these

01:04:45.090 --> 01:04:46.548 align:middle line:84%
in the problem set,
but we'll start

01:04:46.548 --> 01:04:50.280 align:middle line:90%
constructing PBE in this game.

01:04:50.280 --> 01:04:51.680 align:middle line:90%
Perfect Bayesian Equilibria.

01:04:51.680 --> 01:04:53.620 align:middle line:84%
And as usual, we're going
to separate into two cases.

01:04:53.620 --> 01:04:55.495 align:middle line:84%
We're going to look for
a pooling equilibrium

01:04:55.495 --> 01:04:57.120 align:middle line:90%
and a separating equilibrium.

01:04:57.120 --> 01:05:00.960 align:middle line:84%
So on the one hand,
we'll do pooling,

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:02.300 align:middle line:90%
and the other is separating.

01:05:02.300 --> 01:05:09.960 align:middle line:90%


01:05:09.960 --> 01:05:12.800 align:middle line:84%
A pooling equilibrium
means sellers

01:05:12.800 --> 01:05:14.640 align:middle line:84%
with high-quality
products and sellers

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:17.480 align:middle line:84%
with low-quality products
both sell at the same price.

01:05:17.480 --> 01:05:19.160 align:middle line:84%
So the price alone
doesn't give away

01:05:19.160 --> 01:05:21.040 align:middle line:84%
whether it's high
quality or low quality.

01:05:21.040 --> 01:05:24.060 align:middle line:84%
So in a pooling equilibrium,
the seller's strategy--

01:05:24.060 --> 01:05:27.730 align:middle line:90%


01:05:27.730 --> 01:05:29.980 align:middle line:84%
so let's make sure we
understand what's going on here.

01:05:29.980 --> 01:05:31.780 align:middle line:84%
What is the seller's
strategy in this game?

01:05:31.780 --> 01:05:35.240 align:middle line:84%
We need to say if the quality
is low, what price do they set?

01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:38.120 align:middle line:84%
And if the quality is high,
what price do they set?

01:05:38.120 --> 01:05:40.820 align:middle line:84%
In a pooling equilibrium, these
are going to be the same price,

01:05:40.820 --> 01:05:42.350 align:middle line:84%
and we'll call
that price p star.

01:05:42.350 --> 01:05:44.600 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be a
parameter of the equilibrium

01:05:44.600 --> 01:05:47.070 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to
try to solve for.

01:05:47.070 --> 01:05:48.290 align:middle line:90%
What is the buyer's strategy?

01:05:48.290 --> 01:05:51.750 align:middle line:84%
As I say, I think writing
down the strategies correctly

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:53.730 align:middle line:84%
is often the hardest
part of this.

01:05:53.730 --> 01:05:56.330 align:middle line:84%
So what is the strategy
for the buyer in this game?

01:05:56.330 --> 01:06:02.350 align:middle line:90%


01:06:02.350 --> 01:06:04.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can't you
find an expected value

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:07.710 align:middle line:90%
of the quality in some way?

01:06:07.710 --> 01:06:08.350 align:middle line:90%
Or--

01:06:08.350 --> 01:06:09.750 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You could--

01:06:09.750 --> 01:06:13.210 align:middle line:84%
you could, but let's not
even think about optimality.

01:06:13.210 --> 01:06:15.490 align:middle line:84%
Let's just think what kind
of object is a strategy?

01:06:15.490 --> 01:06:17.550 align:middle line:84%
It's a number, is it a
vector, is it a function?

01:06:17.550 --> 01:06:19.355 align:middle line:90%
Just what thing is it?

01:06:19.355 --> 01:06:20.730 align:middle line:84%
What kind of
mathematical object?

01:06:20.730 --> 01:06:21.730 align:middle line:84%
I think that's often
the first step.

01:06:21.730 --> 01:06:22.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, in the back.

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:24.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would it be
either accept or reject

01:06:24.510 --> 01:06:25.880 align:middle line:90%
as a function of the price?

01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:26.630 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:06:26.630 --> 01:06:28.210 align:middle line:84%
As a function of the price
of the seller offers.

01:06:28.210 --> 01:06:28.430 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:06:28.430 --> 01:06:30.110 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be
a function-- maybe

01:06:30.110 --> 01:06:33.030 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it q since we think
of q as quantity of buying.

01:06:33.030 --> 01:06:38.897 align:middle line:84%
q from maybe-- if we don't
restrict the price at all, well,

01:06:38.897 --> 01:06:40.730 align:middle line:84%
the seller can offer
any non-negative price,

01:06:40.730 --> 01:06:46.930 align:middle line:84%
say between 0 and infinity,
not inclusive, to--

01:06:46.930 --> 01:06:49.810 align:middle line:90%
well, maybe we'll call 0, 1.

01:06:49.810 --> 01:06:52.230 align:middle line:84%
So q, we can think of this as
the quantity that they buy.

01:06:52.230 --> 01:06:54.570 align:middle line:84%
They either buy 0, that means
they don't buy or reject,

01:06:54.570 --> 01:06:57.110 align:middle line:84%
or q equals 1, that means
they buy or accept the offer.

01:06:57.110 --> 01:07:00.330 align:middle line:90%


01:07:00.330 --> 01:07:06.250 align:middle line:90%
And let's put our graph here.

01:07:06.250 --> 01:07:09.870 align:middle line:90%
We have vL, we have vH.

01:07:09.870 --> 01:07:12.450 align:middle line:90%


01:07:12.450 --> 01:07:16.250 align:middle line:84%
And I want to try to
work up gradually and try

01:07:16.250 --> 01:07:19.210 align:middle line:90%
to build up an equilibrium.

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:22.215 align:middle line:84%
So the first, I think,
useful observation

01:07:22.215 --> 01:07:24.090 align:middle line:84%
when we're trying to
pin down the equilibrium

01:07:24.090 --> 01:07:27.250 align:middle line:84%
is to say, well, there
are some prices where

01:07:27.250 --> 01:07:31.225 align:middle line:84%
we know what the buyer has to
do, whatever their belief is.

01:07:31.225 --> 01:07:31.850 align:middle line:90%
So let's think.

01:07:31.850 --> 01:07:34.330 align:middle line:84%
The buyer's belief is
going to form a belief

01:07:34.330 --> 01:07:35.890 align:middle line:90%
about the quality of the item.

01:07:35.890 --> 01:07:38.370 align:middle line:84%
And if they're
really pessimistic,

01:07:38.370 --> 01:07:40.548 align:middle line:84%
they would think the
item is worth vL.

01:07:40.548 --> 01:07:42.090 align:middle line:84%
If they're really
optimistic, they're

01:07:42.090 --> 01:07:44.120 align:middle line:84%
going to think the
item is worth vH.

01:07:44.120 --> 01:07:45.640 align:middle line:84%
And if their beliefs
vary, they're

01:07:45.640 --> 01:07:47.860 align:middle line:84%
going to value the item
somewhere between vL and vH

01:07:47.860 --> 01:07:49.943 align:middle line:84%
depending on how likely
they think it is that it's

01:07:49.943 --> 01:07:51.440 align:middle line:90%
high quality or low quality.

01:07:51.440 --> 01:07:53.617 align:middle line:84%
But if they get offered
a price below vL,

01:07:53.617 --> 01:07:54.700 align:middle line:90%
what are they going to do?

01:07:54.700 --> 01:07:58.120 align:middle line:90%


01:07:58.120 --> 01:08:00.280 align:middle line:84%
They have to buy
because even if they're

01:08:00.280 --> 01:08:03.600 align:middle line:84%
certain the good is low quality,
this price is still lower

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:05.440 align:middle line:90%
than their value for the good.

01:08:05.440 --> 01:08:08.190 align:middle line:84%
What if they see
a price above vH?

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:09.440 align:middle line:90%
They're never going to buy it.

01:08:09.440 --> 01:08:10.960 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't matter-- even if
they're certain the good is

01:08:10.960 --> 01:08:13.080 align:middle line:84%
high quality, it's not
worth buying because vH

01:08:13.080 --> 01:08:14.260 align:middle line:90%
is smaller than the price.

01:08:14.260 --> 01:08:15.800 align:middle line:90%
So we immediately know that--

01:08:15.800 --> 01:08:17.920 align:middle line:90%
let's think of q over here.

01:08:17.920 --> 01:08:24.520 align:middle line:84%
We're immediately going
to have 1 here and 0 here.

01:08:24.520 --> 01:08:27.200 align:middle line:84%
And the flexibility is going
to be between vL and vH.

01:08:27.200 --> 01:08:29.475 align:middle line:84%
For a price between vL
and vH, whether they

01:08:29.475 --> 01:08:31.100 align:middle line:84%
buy is going to depend
on their belief,

01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:32.760 align:middle line:84%
because if they're certain
it's high quality--

01:08:32.760 --> 01:08:34.319 align:middle line:84%
let's say there's
a price in here.

01:08:34.319 --> 01:08:36.920 align:middle line:84%
If they're certain it's high
quality, they do want to buy,

01:08:36.920 --> 01:08:39.420 align:middle line:84%
if they're certain it's low
quality, they don't want to buy,

01:08:39.420 --> 01:08:43.870 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to depend
on what their belief is.

01:08:43.870 --> 01:08:46.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:08:46.830 --> 01:08:50.149 align:middle line:84%
What else can we say when we're
looking for an equilibrium?

01:08:50.149 --> 01:08:54.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, remember,
PBE, we have to have

01:08:54.390 --> 01:08:56.569 align:middle line:84%
sequential optimality
for the seller,

01:08:56.569 --> 01:08:58.149 align:middle line:84%
sequential optimality
for the buyer,

01:08:58.149 --> 01:09:00.550 align:middle line:84%
and then Bayes'
consistency for the buyer.

01:09:00.550 --> 01:09:03.612 align:middle line:84%
Now Bayes' consistency only
pins down the buyer's beliefs

01:09:03.612 --> 01:09:05.029 align:middle line:84%
when they see a
price that they're

01:09:05.029 --> 01:09:06.330 align:middle line:90%
supposed to see in equilibrium.

01:09:06.330 --> 01:09:08.310 align:middle line:84%
It only pins things
down on-path.

01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:12.950 align:middle line:84%
So what is going to be
on-path in this equilibrium?

01:09:12.950 --> 01:09:17.310 align:middle line:84%
What price are they expecting
to see in this equilibrium?

01:09:17.310 --> 01:09:18.710 align:middle line:90%
Just p star.

01:09:18.710 --> 01:09:20.910 align:middle line:84%
That's the only price
they should see.

01:09:20.910 --> 01:09:23.170 align:middle line:84%
Because that's what the
low type seller is offering

01:09:23.170 --> 01:09:25.212 align:middle line:84%
and that's what the high
type seller is offering.

01:09:25.212 --> 01:09:28.350 align:middle line:84%
So if they see a
price of p star--

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:33.492 align:middle line:84%
so let's-- what
belief do they form?

01:09:33.492 --> 01:09:34.450 align:middle line:90%
They apply Bayes' rule.

01:09:34.450 --> 01:09:36.325 align:middle line:84%
What happens when they
see a price of p star?

01:09:36.325 --> 01:09:41.090 align:middle line:90%


01:09:41.090 --> 01:09:42.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:09:42.170 --> 01:09:44.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We would
have been given pi,

01:09:44.270 --> 01:09:46.970 align:middle line:84%
we would have an
intuition about is it

01:09:46.970 --> 01:09:49.010 align:middle line:84%
low type or high
type, and then-- like,

01:09:49.010 --> 01:09:52.569 align:middle line:84%
we would know which one of those
is higher probability mitigating

01:09:52.569 --> 01:09:54.943 align:middle line:84%
whether the car is actually
low type or high type.

01:09:54.943 --> 01:09:56.610 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, but
here, we're looking

01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:57.750 align:middle line:90%
for a pooling equilibrium.

01:09:57.750 --> 01:10:00.570 align:middle line:84%
Where both the high-type seller
and-- both the low-type seller

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:02.610 align:middle line:84%
and the high-type seller
sell it at p star.

01:10:02.610 --> 01:10:06.330 align:middle line:84%
So when I see the price of
p star, what is my belief?

01:10:06.330 --> 01:10:07.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:09.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That it's high
type of probability pi.

01:10:09.170 --> 01:10:09.430 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

01:10:09.430 --> 01:10:10.950 align:middle line:90%
It's just the same as the prior.

01:10:10.950 --> 01:10:13.970 align:middle line:84%
So if everyone is charging
a price of p star,

01:10:13.970 --> 01:10:15.630 align:middle line:90%
I keep my beliefs.

01:10:15.630 --> 01:10:18.047 align:middle line:84%
My beliefs stay exactly
what they were at the prior.

01:10:18.047 --> 01:10:19.630 align:middle line:84%
I have no reason to
update my belief--

01:10:19.630 --> 01:10:21.350 align:middle line:84%
I mean, formally I'm
applying Bayes' rule,

01:10:21.350 --> 01:10:22.850 align:middle line:84%
but when I update
my beliefs, they're

01:10:22.850 --> 01:10:24.690 align:middle line:84%
the same as they were
before because everyone

01:10:24.690 --> 01:10:25.815 align:middle line:90%
offers the price of p star.

01:10:25.815 --> 01:10:27.570 align:middle line:84%
The price doesn't
really reveal anything.

01:10:27.570 --> 01:10:38.160 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll say p
star belief pi on H.

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:43.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, if I believe the item
is high with probability pi,

01:10:43.480 --> 01:10:46.440 align:middle line:84%
then the value I assign to it--
maybe I'll call the value v

01:10:46.440 --> 01:10:47.240 align:middle line:90%
star--

01:10:47.240 --> 01:10:54.080 align:middle line:84%
is going to be pi times vH
plus 1 minus pi times vL.

01:10:54.080 --> 01:10:57.600 align:middle line:84%
That's my expected
valuation of the item given

01:10:57.600 --> 01:10:59.140 align:middle line:90%
that my belief here is pi.

01:10:59.140 --> 01:11:01.960 align:middle line:90%


01:11:01.960 --> 01:11:03.620 align:middle line:84%
So we're kind of
building this up.

01:11:03.620 --> 01:11:05.995 align:middle line:84%
We're looking for an equilibrium
where the seller charges

01:11:05.995 --> 01:11:06.760 align:middle line:90%
a price of p star.

01:11:06.760 --> 01:11:09.600 align:middle line:84%
We know that the buyer's
valuation has to be this.

01:11:09.600 --> 01:11:12.520 align:middle line:84%
And we want the seller to sell
the item at a price of p star.

01:11:12.520 --> 01:11:14.600 align:middle line:84%
So what must be
true about p star

01:11:14.600 --> 01:11:18.360 align:middle line:84%
relative to v star
in this equilibrium?

01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:19.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:11:19.160 --> 01:11:20.480 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: p star equals v star?

01:11:20.480 --> 01:11:21.440 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It could
equal v star, but it

01:11:21.440 --> 01:11:23.357 align:middle line:84%
has to be at least weakly
smaller than v star.

01:11:23.357 --> 01:11:24.980 align:middle line:90%
You might-- it could be v star.

01:11:24.980 --> 01:11:27.920 align:middle line:90%
So let's put v star in here.

01:11:27.920 --> 01:11:32.520 align:middle line:84%
And p star is
going to have to be

01:11:32.520 --> 01:11:36.350 align:middle line:90%
somewhere smaller than v star.

01:11:36.350 --> 01:11:40.027 align:middle line:84%
And we know that at
a price of p star,

01:11:40.027 --> 01:11:42.110 align:middle line:84%
let's look for an equilibrium
where the buyer does

01:11:42.110 --> 01:11:44.790 align:middle line:90%
buy at a price of p star.

01:11:44.790 --> 01:11:46.990 align:middle line:84%
Now the trick for
these equilibria

01:11:46.990 --> 01:11:49.430 align:middle line:84%
is often you want
to make deviations

01:11:49.430 --> 01:11:50.810 align:middle line:90%
as unattractive as possible.

01:11:50.810 --> 01:11:52.510 align:middle line:84%
So we want to make
this an equilibrium.

01:11:52.510 --> 01:11:55.177 align:middle line:84%
That means we want to make sure
the seller doesn't want to offer

01:11:55.177 --> 01:11:56.850 align:middle line:90%
a price different from p star.

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:58.350 align:middle line:84%
To make sure the
seller doesn't want

01:11:58.350 --> 01:12:00.350 align:middle line:84%
to offer a price
different than p star,

01:12:00.350 --> 01:12:03.070 align:middle line:84%
it better be the buyer never
buys if a price is strictly

01:12:03.070 --> 01:12:04.847 align:middle line:90%
higher than p star.

01:12:04.847 --> 01:12:06.930 align:middle line:84%
So if I see a price higher
than p star as a buyer,

01:12:06.930 --> 01:12:08.847 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to buy,
and that way, the seller

01:12:08.847 --> 01:12:10.130 align:middle line:90%
won't want to deviate to this.

01:12:10.130 --> 01:12:13.650 align:middle line:84%
So let's look for an
equilibrium that goes like this.

01:12:13.650 --> 01:12:17.790 align:middle line:90%


01:12:17.790 --> 01:12:20.630 align:middle line:84%
Seems like a
natural equilibrium.

01:12:20.630 --> 01:12:25.750 align:middle line:84%
If I see any price that's weakly
below p star as a buyer, I buy.

01:12:25.750 --> 01:12:30.750 align:middle line:84%
And if I see any price
above p star, I don't buy.

01:12:30.750 --> 01:12:32.090 align:middle line:90%
Now we have to specify beliefs.

01:12:32.090 --> 01:12:35.460 align:middle line:84%
I think the easiest
thing is to say--

01:12:35.460 --> 01:12:43.860 align:middle line:84%
well, let's say--
it doesn't even--

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:46.360 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't even matter, but
sometimes this is a good trick.

01:12:46.360 --> 01:12:48.900 align:middle line:90%


01:12:48.900 --> 01:12:51.452 align:middle line:84%
Let's say that if I see any
price strictly below p star,

01:12:51.452 --> 01:12:53.660 align:middle line:84%
here, I have a lot of
flexibility in what the beliefs

01:12:53.660 --> 01:12:57.242 align:middle line:84%
are because we're off-path, but
I know that the buyer is buying,

01:12:57.242 --> 01:12:58.700 align:middle line:84%
so to make that
optimal, let's just

01:12:58.700 --> 01:13:01.040 align:middle line:84%
say they believe the good is
certain to be high quality.

01:13:01.040 --> 01:13:03.260 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't really
matter what I do here.

01:13:03.260 --> 01:13:06.060 align:middle line:84%
If they see a price strictly
greater than p star,

01:13:06.060 --> 01:13:08.740 align:middle line:84%
they believe it's low
quality and don't buy.

01:13:08.740 --> 01:13:11.953 align:middle line:84%
And if they see a value--
a price of exactly p star,

01:13:11.953 --> 01:13:13.620 align:middle line:84%
well, we know what
they have to believe.

01:13:13.620 --> 01:13:16.860 align:middle line:84%
They have to believe the good is
high quality with probability pi

01:13:16.860 --> 01:13:19.500 align:middle line:84%
because this price is on-path,
and therefore, Bayes' rule

01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:20.980 align:middle line:90%
can be applied.

01:13:20.980 --> 01:13:23.280 align:middle line:84%
For these lower prices
and these higher prices,

01:13:23.280 --> 01:13:25.620 align:middle line:84%
I'm totally flexible in how
I choose the buyer's beliefs

01:13:25.620 --> 01:13:28.240 align:middle line:84%
because these are off-path, and
if I try to apply Bayes' rule,

01:13:28.240 --> 01:13:30.198 align:middle line:84%
I would divide by 0, and
therefore, Bayes' rule

01:13:30.198 --> 01:13:31.180 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have any bite.

01:13:31.180 --> 01:13:34.800 align:middle line:90%


01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:37.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I think we're
good, but I think

01:13:37.240 --> 01:13:38.580 align:middle line:90%
we need one more constraint.

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:41.800 align:middle line:90%
So we've defined p star.

01:13:41.800 --> 01:13:45.160 align:middle line:84%
We've said p star is
between vL and v star.

01:13:45.160 --> 01:13:47.560 align:middle line:84%
This means that the
buyer is willing to buy.

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:50.263 align:middle line:84%
I think there's one
more potential issue

01:13:50.263 --> 01:13:52.180 align:middle line:84%
with this equilibrium,
so let's go through it.

01:13:52.180 --> 01:13:55.800 align:middle line:84%
We've checked-- maybe
I'll just write this here.

01:13:55.800 --> 01:14:00.280 align:middle line:84%
We've checked belief consistency
because here, the buyer

01:14:00.280 --> 01:14:01.680 align:middle line:90%
believes it's low quality.

01:14:01.680 --> 01:14:04.640 align:middle line:84%
They believe it's high quality,
they certainly want to buy.

01:14:04.640 --> 01:14:06.140 align:middle line:84%
Here, they believe
it's low quality,

01:14:06.140 --> 01:14:08.120 align:middle line:84%
they certainly
don't want to buy.

01:14:08.120 --> 01:14:11.640 align:middle line:84%
Here, they believe its
expected quality is v star

01:14:11.640 --> 01:14:14.720 align:middle line:84%
and they're, therefore, willing
to buy at a price of p star.

01:14:14.720 --> 01:14:17.360 align:middle line:90%
So Bayes' consistency is right.

01:14:17.360 --> 01:14:21.840 align:middle line:84%
Receiver optimality, RO-- ah,
receiver-- buyer optimality

01:14:21.840 --> 01:14:23.520 align:middle line:84%
is good, but I think
there's one more

01:14:23.520 --> 01:14:25.180 align:middle line:90%
issue with seller optimality.

01:14:25.180 --> 01:14:27.240 align:middle line:84%
So we have to check,
does the seller ever

01:14:27.240 --> 01:14:30.890 align:middle line:84%
want to deviate to
offering a different price?

01:14:30.890 --> 01:14:34.595 align:middle line:90%


01:14:34.595 --> 01:14:35.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, the trick
is always to say,

01:14:35.970 --> 01:14:41.570 align:middle line:84%
what is the most
attractive deviation?

01:14:41.570 --> 01:14:43.870 align:middle line:84%
Would the seller ever
want to charge a price--

01:14:43.870 --> 01:14:45.200 align:middle line:90%
a lower price here?

01:14:45.200 --> 01:14:47.450 align:middle line:84%
No, because they're already
selling it with certainty.

01:14:47.450 --> 01:14:48.992 align:middle line:84%
If they offered a
lower price, they'd

01:14:48.992 --> 01:14:50.588 align:middle line:90%
still style it with certainty.

01:14:50.588 --> 01:14:52.130 align:middle line:84%
But what might the
seller want to do?

01:14:52.130 --> 01:14:55.310 align:middle line:90%
What do we have to check?

01:14:55.310 --> 01:15:00.310 align:middle line:84%
Well, does the seller actually
want to sell it at price p star?

01:15:00.310 --> 01:15:02.322 align:middle line:84%
Now the issue is, there's
two types of sellers.

01:15:02.322 --> 01:15:04.530 align:middle line:84%
There's the high-type seller
and the low-type seller.

01:15:04.530 --> 01:15:07.343 align:middle line:84%
We have to say, what is the
more tempting deviation?

01:15:07.343 --> 01:15:09.510 align:middle line:84%
The issue we have to worry
about is-- and maybe I'll

01:15:09.510 --> 01:15:11.890 align:middle line:90%
do separating on another page.

01:15:11.890 --> 01:15:16.390 align:middle line:90%


01:15:16.390 --> 01:15:19.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at both cases.

01:15:19.510 --> 01:15:27.420 align:middle line:84%
The high-type seller is
comparing p star minus cH.

01:15:27.420 --> 01:15:32.980 align:middle line:90%


01:15:32.980 --> 01:15:36.420 align:middle line:90%
These are the two constraints.

01:15:36.420 --> 01:15:40.500 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a high-type seller, I
could charge a price of p star,

01:15:40.500 --> 01:15:44.380 align:middle line:84%
and I sell at p star, but I
have to pay, but my cost is cH.

01:15:44.380 --> 01:15:47.020 align:middle line:84%
Or I could set a price
that no one will buy it,

01:15:47.020 --> 01:15:49.340 align:middle line:90%
and therefore, I get 0.

01:15:49.340 --> 01:15:51.380 align:middle line:84%
Conversely, if I'm
the low-type seller,

01:15:51.380 --> 01:15:53.517 align:middle line:84%
I could set a price of
p star, pay my cost,

01:15:53.517 --> 01:15:55.100 align:middle line:84%
or I could sell at
a really high price

01:15:55.100 --> 01:15:56.540 align:middle line:90%
that no one's going to buy it.

01:15:56.540 --> 01:15:58.280 align:middle line:84%
Which of these is
harder to satisfy?

01:15:58.280 --> 01:16:01.500 align:middle line:90%


01:16:01.500 --> 01:16:04.820 align:middle line:84%
The top one, because the
high-type seller finds

01:16:04.820 --> 01:16:05.920 align:middle line:90%
it more costly to produce.

01:16:05.920 --> 01:16:07.420 align:middle line:84%
So they might say
this price p star,

01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:08.763 align:middle line:90%
it's not really worth it for me.

01:16:08.763 --> 01:16:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be
the binding constraint.

01:16:10.680 --> 01:16:13.300 align:middle line:84%
This is the critical--
the important constraint.

01:16:13.300 --> 01:16:15.140 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to
be the final constraint

01:16:15.140 --> 01:16:18.020 align:middle line:90%
that we need to impose.

01:16:18.020 --> 01:16:23.220 align:middle line:84%
So what we need, to
write it in words,

01:16:23.220 --> 01:16:36.200 align:middle line:84%
is we need vL and cH less than
or equal to p star less than

01:16:36.200 --> 01:16:38.140 align:middle line:90%
or equal to v star.

01:16:38.140 --> 01:16:53.080 align:middle line:90%


01:16:53.080 --> 01:16:55.960 align:middle line:84%
This inequality says
the buyer is actually

01:16:55.960 --> 01:16:59.280 align:middle line:84%
willing to buy at
a price of p star.

01:16:59.280 --> 01:17:01.905 align:middle line:84%
This inequality says even
when the seller knows

01:17:01.905 --> 01:17:03.280 align:middle line:84%
the good is high
quality, they're

01:17:03.280 --> 01:17:06.400 align:middle line:84%
still willing to sell
at a price of p star.

01:17:06.400 --> 01:17:09.080 align:middle line:84%
And the vL inequality
is important

01:17:09.080 --> 01:17:13.080 align:middle line:84%
because if p star were
less than vL, then

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:15.080 align:middle line:84%
the seller could always
slightly raise the price

01:17:15.080 --> 01:17:16.622 align:middle line:84%
and the buyer would
still always have

01:17:16.622 --> 01:17:18.880 align:middle line:84%
to buy because we know
the buyer always buys

01:17:18.880 --> 01:17:20.600 align:middle line:90%
when the price is below vL.

01:17:20.600 --> 01:17:22.780 align:middle line:84%
So these are the three
inequalities that we get.

01:17:22.780 --> 01:17:25.230 align:middle line:84%
And I think when you go through
and try to solve for PBE,

01:17:25.230 --> 01:17:30.190 align:middle line:84%
these are the steps
you have to go through.

01:17:30.190 --> 01:17:33.630 align:middle line:84%
Let me say just a little bit
about the separating equilibrium

01:17:33.630 --> 01:17:36.070 align:middle line:90%
in the last two minutes.

01:17:36.070 --> 01:17:37.350 align:middle line:90%
So that was pooling.

01:17:37.350 --> 01:17:39.850 align:middle line:84%
Let's go down here
to separating.

01:17:39.850 --> 01:17:49.270 align:middle line:90%


01:17:49.270 --> 01:17:55.830 align:middle line:84%
So for the separating
equilibrium,

01:17:55.830 --> 01:17:59.790 align:middle line:90%
we now have pL not equal to pH.

01:17:59.790 --> 01:18:01.910 align:middle line:84%
So when the seller knows
the good is low quality,

01:18:01.910 --> 01:18:03.022 align:middle line:90%
they sell it at pL.

01:18:03.022 --> 01:18:05.230 align:middle line:84%
When they know it's high
quality, they sell it at pH.

01:18:05.230 --> 01:18:06.910 align:middle line:90%
These are different.

01:18:06.910 --> 01:18:10.830 align:middle line:84%
As before, the buyer is
choosing a strategy q

01:18:10.830 --> 01:18:15.150 align:middle line:90%
from 0, infinity to 0, 1.

01:18:15.150 --> 01:18:17.870 align:middle line:90%
Now, which prices are on-path?

01:18:17.870 --> 01:18:19.790 align:middle line:90%
It's both pL and pH.

01:18:19.790 --> 01:18:21.170 align:middle line:90%
So now let's look at beliefs.

01:18:21.170 --> 01:18:26.420 align:middle line:90%


01:18:26.420 --> 01:18:28.620 align:middle line:84%
If the buyer sees
a price of pL, they

01:18:28.620 --> 01:18:31.660 align:middle line:84%
know that the seller
is low quality, so they

01:18:31.660 --> 01:18:36.460 align:middle line:90%
assign probability 0 to h.

01:18:36.460 --> 01:18:41.340 align:middle line:84%
If they see pH, they know the
seller is high quality, so they

01:18:41.340 --> 01:18:46.260 align:middle line:90%
assign probability 1 to h.

01:18:46.260 --> 01:18:51.792 align:middle line:84%
And if they see any
other price, well,

01:18:51.792 --> 01:18:53.500 align:middle line:84%
now their beliefs are
flexible because we

01:18:53.500 --> 01:18:56.440 align:middle line:84%
can't apply Bayes' rule, this
is off-path, so flexible.

01:18:56.440 --> 01:19:08.300 align:middle line:90%


01:19:08.300 --> 01:19:11.125 align:middle line:84%
And we can go through and
try to do the same analysis.

01:19:11.125 --> 01:19:13.000 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we'll just say
two kind of tricks here.

01:19:13.000 --> 01:19:16.540 align:middle line:90%


01:19:16.540 --> 01:19:18.600 align:middle line:84%
You might look-- let me
just say one final thing.

01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:21.080 align:middle line:84%
You might try to find an
equilibrium where the buyer buys

01:19:21.080 --> 01:19:22.940 align:middle line:90%
at price pL and at pH.

01:19:22.940 --> 01:19:24.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to argue that
can't be in equilibrium.

01:19:24.940 --> 01:19:26.120 align:middle line:84%
Why could there not
be an equilibrium

01:19:26.120 --> 01:19:27.960 align:middle line:84%
where the buyer buys
when they see pL

01:19:27.960 --> 01:19:29.100 align:middle line:90%
and buys when they see pH?

01:19:29.100 --> 01:19:30.892 align:middle line:84%
Why can't there be a
separating equilibrium

01:19:30.892 --> 01:19:32.140 align:middle line:90%
where the buyer always buys?

01:19:32.140 --> 01:19:38.600 align:middle line:90%


01:19:38.600 --> 01:19:41.080 align:middle line:84%
Think about the
seller's incentives.

01:19:41.080 --> 01:19:45.080 align:middle line:84%
There are two prices at
which the seller always buys.

01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:46.420 align:middle line:90%
What would you do as the seller?

01:19:46.420 --> 01:19:48.960 align:middle line:90%


01:19:48.960 --> 01:19:51.420 align:middle line:90%
Well, one of these is higher.

01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:52.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's say pH is higher.

01:19:52.540 --> 01:19:54.040 align:middle line:84%
If you were the
seller, you'd always

01:19:54.040 --> 01:19:56.160 align:middle line:84%
sell at pH because
you'd get more revenue

01:19:56.160 --> 01:19:57.663 align:middle line:90%
and the buyer is willing to buy.

01:19:57.663 --> 01:19:59.080 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out,
there's not going

01:19:59.080 --> 01:20:01.538 align:middle line:84%
to be a separating equilibrium
where the buyer always buys.

01:20:01.538 --> 01:20:03.997 align:middle line:84%
We're going to actually have
to have a mixed strategy where

01:20:03.997 --> 01:20:06.620 align:middle line:84%
the buyer buys with some
probability following pH,

01:20:06.620 --> 01:20:09.465 align:middle line:84%
but you'll see how to work
through that in the problem set.

01:20:09.465 --> 01:20:10.840 align:middle line:84%
But let's make
sure we understand

01:20:10.840 --> 01:20:11.798 align:middle line:90%
the intuition for that.

01:20:11.798 --> 01:20:12.800 align:middle line:90%
OK, let me stop there.

01:20:12.800 --> 01:20:14.950 align:middle line:90%
I'll see you on Thursday.

01:20:14.950 --> 01:20:18.000 align:middle line:90%